<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch6-inductors">
	<title>Inductors and RL Circuits</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>Inductors are circuit elements that are capable of storing and releasing energy in the form of a magnetic field. Most inductors are composed of a coil of wire, possibly coiled around a ferrite core. (Even a straight wire can create a magnetic field, but the effect is very small when compared to coiled wire inductors.) As current flows through the inductor, a magnetic field is generated.</p>

		<p>The property of an inductor that relates to its ability to store magnetic field energy is known as inductance (<m>L</m>) which is measured with a unit of H (henry). </p>

		<remark>
			<title>Summary of inductance</title>

			<p>
				<ul>
					<li><p>Symbol: <m>L</m></p></li>
					<li><p>Unit: H (henry)</p></li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</remark>

		<p>The construction of inductors takes on many forms, and the equation for inductance in a device depends on the construction and geometry of that particular inductor. For a cylindrical coil of wire, the inductance depends on the permeability of the core material (<m>\mu</m>), the number of turns of wire (<m>N</m>), the area of the coil (<m>A</m>), and the length of the coil (<m>l</m>), as described in <xref ref="eqch6-inductorcoil" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="eqch6-inductorcoil">L = \frac{\mu N^2A}{l}</md>
		</p>

		<p>Many geometries can be used for inductors, and they can contain an air core or a ferrite core depending on how high the inductance requirements are. Straight coils of wire (known as solenoids) are relatively simple to construct but are not as efficient as toroidal designs that are able to keep more magnetic flux isolated inside of the inductor.</p>

		<p>The permeability of a material refers to how much an object magnetizes in response to an applied magnetic field. It is equal to the permeability of free space multiplied by the relative permeability of a particular material, defined by <xref ref="eq-ch6muequation" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="eq-ch6muequation">\mu = \mu_0\mu_r</md>
		</p>

		<p>The relative permeability of materials can be looked up, but is equal to one for free space and approximately 200,000 for ferromagnetic materials such as iron. The permeability of free space is given in <xref ref="eq-ch6mu0" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="eq-ch6mu0">\mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7}~\textrm{H/m}</md>
		</p>

		<p>The circuit symbol for an inductor is shown in <xref ref="inductorsymbol" />. Just as the capacitor symbol looks like parallel plates, the inductor symbol resembles a coil of wire.</p>

		<figure xml:id="inductorsymbol">
  			<caption>The circuit symbol for an inductor.</caption>
			<image width="30%" xml:id="ch6-inductorsymbol">
				<shortdescription>Circuit symbol for an inductor which resembles a coil of wire.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw (0,2) to [L] (3,2);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>Variable inductors are not common but do exist. One type of variable inductor construction is to have a straight coil of wire with a moveable ferrite core. As the ferrite core is moved into the coil, the inductance increases. As the ferrite core is moved out of the coil, the inductance decreases.</p>

  	</introduction>

  	<section xml:id="ch6-parasitic">
		<title>Parasitic Effects</title>

		<p>Inductors are made of coils of wire. To create a high inductance device, a lot of wire is required. This generates parasitic side-effects. Wire contains resistance which, while it can be safely ignored when small amounts are used, can no longer be ignored when large amounts are used. To create physically small inductors, wire with a smaller surface area (high gauge) are used, which increases the resistance of the wire. The resistance of an inductor is known as <term>parasitic resistance.</term> In addition, there can be <term>parasitic capacitance</term> in an inductor due to the presence of charge in many parallel wires. These parasitic effects may vary with the frequency of the applied signal. A model of an inductor, including parasitic resistance and capacitance, is shown in <xref ref="parasiticinductor" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="parasiticinductor">
  			<caption>An inductor model including parasitic resistance (<m>R_P</m>) and parasitic capacitance (<m>C_P</m>).</caption>
			<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-parasiticinductor">
				<shortdescription>An inductor L is in series with a resistor Rp. Those two circuit elements are in parallel with a capacitor Cp.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw[]	(0,0) to [L, l^=$L$] (2,0);
						\draw[]	(2,0) to [R, l^=$R_P$] (4,0);
						\draw[]	(0,-1.25) to [C, l^=$C_P$] (4,-1.25);
						\draw[]	(0,0) --++ (down:1.25);
						\draw[]	(4,0) --++ (down:1.25);
						\draw[]	(0,-0.625) to [short, *-] ++ (left:1);
						\draw[]	(4,-0.625) to [short, *-] ++ (right:1);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>This textbook will ignore parasitic capacitance. At times, we will consider parasitic resistance. Any examples that include parasitic resistance will be explicitly described. It is important to understand that the parasitic effects of an inductor (particularly parasitic resistance) are non-trivial in real-world applications.</p>
			
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch6-measuringinductance">
		<title>Measuring Inductance</title>

		<p>Inductors generally cannot be measured with a digital multimeter, as the inclusion of the necessary components to measure inductance would increase the cost prohibitively. They can, however, be measured with LRC meters. Much like with a capacitor, an LRC meter applies an AC voltage, measures the AC current, and uses the phase and amplitude differences to calculate the inductance.</p>

		<p>Inductance must be measured from one end of the inductor (or equivalent inductance) to the other, in parallel, as shown in <xref ref="measuringinductance" />.</p>

		<figure xml:id="measuringinductance">
  			<caption>A diagram of how inductance is measured in parallel (across a component or components).</caption>
			<image width="30%" xml:id="ch5-measuringinductance">
				<shortdescription>An inductance meter is shown with + and - terminals. The circuit symbol for an inductor is shown. A wire connects the + side of the inductance meter to one of the wires of the inductor. A second wire connects the - side of the inductance meter to the other wire of the inductor.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw (0.5,1.5) to [qvprobe] (3.5,1.5);
						\draw[-Stealth] (0.5,1.5) -- (0.5,0);
						\draw[-Stealth] (3.5,1.5) -- (3.5,0);
						\draw (0,0) to [L] (4,0);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch6-viinductance">
		<title>Voltage-Current Relationship in an Inductor</title>

		<p>The relationship between voltage and current in an inductor can be derived using Faraday's law of induction. The full derivation will not be shown in this text. The voltage drop over an inductor is given by <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-voltageinductor">v(t) = L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</md>
		</p>

		<p>Based on <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />, if the current remains unchanging through time, there will be no voltage drop over an inductor. Therefore, in DC conditions, an inductor can be replaced by a short circuit. This analysis technique will be used frequently in this book to explore the transient response of resistor-inductor circuits.</p>
		
		<p>The current through an inductor can be found by integrating both sides of <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. The result is given in <xref ref="ch6-currentinductor" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-currentinductor">i(t) = \frac{1}{L}\int_{-\infty}^{t} v(\tau)\,d\tau</md>
		</p>

		<p>Although calculus is required to fully analyze the relationship between voltage and current in an inductor, the relationship between the two variables is linear. This means that inductors are linear circuit elements, and all of the techniques discussed in this book (such as superposition and Th&#233;venin equivalence, for example) are still relevant with inductive circuits.</p>
		
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance">
		<title>Equivalent Inductance</title>
		
		<introduction>

			<p>Just as it was possible to combine resistors together to find an equivalent resistance, and capacitors to find an equivalent capacitance, it is possible to combine inductors together to find an equivalent inductance. The rules (and mathematical relationships) for calculating series and parallel combinations of inductors are explained below.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch6-seriesinductance">
			<title>Series Combinations of Inductors</title>

			<p>Inductors in series all share a common branch, as shown in <xref ref="seriesinductorsdiagram" />. Each individual voltage drop is shown (numbered with subscripts), as well as the total voltage drop over the combination of inductors (<m>v(t)</m>). The current through all of the inductors is the same and is labeled <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="seriesinductorsdiagram">
  				<caption>Five inductors in series in a circuit.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-seriesinductorsdiagram">
					<shortdescription>Each inductor contains its own voltage drop noted v_n(t). The entire voltage drop over all inductors is v(t). The current through all inductors is i(t).</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) to [L, v^=$v_1(t)$] (2,0);
							\draw (2,0) to [L, v^=$v_2(t)$] (4,0);
							\draw (4,0) to [L, v^=$v_3(t)$] (6,0);
							\draw (6,0) to [L, v^=$v_4(t)$] (8,0);
							\draw (8,0) to [L, v^=$v_5(t)$, i_=$i(t)$] (10,0);
							\draw (0,1.5) to [open, v^=$v(t)$] (10,1.5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Because <m>v(t)</m> is equal to the sum of each individual voltage drop, the current-voltage relationship in inductors (described in <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />) can be used to determine the equivalent inductance of series combinations of inductors. This is shown in <xref ref="ch6-eqseriesinductance" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch6-seriesinductanceproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-eqseriesinductance">L_{EQ} = L_1 + L_2 + L_3 + ... + L_n</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-seriesinductanceproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(t) \amp= v_1(t) + v_2(t) + v_3(t) + ... + v_n(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>L_{EQ}\frac{di(t)}{dt} \amp= L_1\frac{di(t)}{dt} + L_2\frac{di(t)}{dt} + L_3\frac{di(t)}{dt} + ... + L_n\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>L_{EQ} \amp= L_1 + L_2 + L_3 + ... + L_n</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Inductors in series therefore combine similarly to resistors in series. The equivalent inductance cannot be smaller than the largest inductor in the series combination.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch6-parallelinductance">
			<title>Parallel Combinations of Inductors</title>

			<p>Inductors in parallel all share two nodes, as shown in <xref ref="parallelinductordiagram" />. Each individual current is shown (numbered with subscripts), as well as the total current through the combination of inductors (<m>i(t)</m>). The voltage drop over all of the inductors is the same and is labeled <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="parallelinductordiagram">
  				<caption>Five inductors in parallel in a circuit.</caption>
				<image width="70%" xml:id="ch6-parallelinductordiagram">
					<shortdescription>Each inductor contains its own current noted i_n(t). The overall current through all inductors is i(t). The voltage drop over all inductors is v(t).</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\def\spacing{1.75}
							\foreach \x in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} {
								\draw[]	(\x*\spacing,0) to [L, i_=$i_\x(t)$] ++(down:2.5);
							}
							\draw[]	(5.5*\spacing,0) to [open, v^=$v(t)$]	++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(3*\spacing,1) to [short, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:1);
							\draw[]	(3*\spacing,-2.5) to [short, *-] ++(down:1);
							\draw[]	(\spacing,0) to [short, -*] ++(right:\spacing) to [short, -*] ++(right:2*\spacing) --++ (right:\spacing);
							\draw[]	(\spacing,-2.5) to [short, -*] ++(right:\spacing) to [short, -*] ++(right:2*\spacing) --++ (right:\spacing);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Because <m>i(t)</m> is equal to the sum of each individual current, the current through an inductor (defined in <xref ref="ch6-currentinductor" />) can be used to determine the equivalent inductance of parallel combinations of inductors. This is shown in <xref ref="ch6-eqparallelinductance" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch6-parallelinductanceproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-eqparallelinductance">\frac{1}{L_{EQ}} = \frac{1}{L_1} + \frac{1}{L_2} + \frac{1}{L_3} + ... + \frac{1}{L_n}</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-parallelinductanceproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>i(t) \amp= i_1(t) + i_2(t) + i_3(t) + ... + i_n(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{L_{EQ}}\int_{-\infty}^{t} v(\tau)\,d\tau \amp= \frac{1}{L_1}\int_{-\infty}^{t} v(\tau)\,d\tau + \frac{1}{L_2}\int_{-\infty}^{t} v(\tau)\,d\tau + \frac{1}{L_3}\int_{-\infty}^{t} v(\tau)\,d\tau + ... + \frac{1}{L_n}\int_{-\infty}^{t} v(\tau)\,d\tau</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{L_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{1}{L_1} + \frac{1}{L_2} + \frac{1}{L_3} + ... + \frac{1}{L_n}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Inductors in parallel therefore combine similarly to resistors in parallel. The equivalent inductance cannot be larger than the smallest inductor in the parallel combination.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating equivalent inductance</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent inductance between nodes a and b.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductanceexamplefig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of six inductors (two 10 mH, two 20mH, one 30mH, and one 40mH). The 10mH (A) and 40mH inductors connect at node a. The 10mH (A), 40mH, and 10mH (B) connect at node c. The 10mH (B), 20mH (B), and 30mH connect at node d. The 20mH (B), 20mH (A), and 30mH connect at node e. The 20mH (A) connects at node b. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,1.875) to [short] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	(2.5,1.875) to [short] ++(up:1.25);
									\draw[]	(0,3.125) to [L, l^=10~mH] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,1.875) to [L, l^=40~mH] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=10~mH, *-] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=20~mH, *-*] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [L, l_=30~mH] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(-1,2.5) node[left] {a} to [short, o-*] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(-1,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] ++(right:1);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [L, l^=20~mH] ++(right:5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine both parallel combinations of inductors. The 10<nbsp />mH and 40<nbsp />mH pair have an equivalent inductance of 8<nbsp />mH.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{(10~\textrm{mH})(40~\textrm{mH})}{10~\textrm{mH} + 40~\textrm{mH}} \amp= \frac{400~\textrm{mH}^2}{50~\textrm{mH}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The 20<nbsp />mH and 30<nbsp />mH pair have an equivalent inductance of 12<nbsp />mH.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{(20~\textrm{mH})(30~\textrm{mH})}{20~\textrm{mH} + 30~\textrm{mH}} \amp= \frac{600~\textrm{mH}^2}{50~\textrm{mH}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit can now be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductanceexamplefig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 0 meshes containing four inductors (8mH, 10mH, 12mH, and 20mH). The 8mH connects at node a. The 8mH and 10mH connect at node c. The 10mH and 12mH connect at node d. The 12mH and 20mH connect at node e. The 20mH connects at node b. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {a} to [L, l^=8~mH, o-] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=10~mH] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l^=12~mH] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [L, l^=20~mH] ++(right:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent inductance can be calculated by summing together the four remaining inductances, which are all connected in series. The equivalent inductance is equal to 50<nbsp />mH.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

	</section>

	

	<section xml:id="ch6-resistor-inductor-circuits">
		<title>Resistor-Inductor (RL) Circuits</title>
		
		<introduction>

			<p>Resistor-inductor (RL) circuits are circuits that contain one inductor (or combinations of inductors that can be reduced down to one equivalent inductance) as well as at least one resistive element. In this chapter, these circuits will be analyzed given an abrupt change from one set of DC conditions to another. The short-term difference in output that occurs as a result of this change in DC conditions is known as a <term>transient analysis</term>.</p>

			<p>Resistors by themselves are incapable of having a transient change. This is because they do not store energy. Because an inductor stores energy, it is able to experience a transient change in response to changing voltage or current conditions of the circuit.</p>
						
			<p>On the other hand, a <term>steady-state analysis</term> considers the operation of a circuit when conditions remain steady for a long period of time. In this chapter, the steady-state analysis will only include DC sources. To analyze the behavior of an inductor in a DC steady-state condition, recall from <xref ref="ch6-viinductance" />:</p>

			<blockquote>
				<p>Based on <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />, if the current remains unchanging through time, there will be no voltage drop over an inductor. Therefore, in DC conditions, an inductor can be replaced by a short circuit.</p>
			</blockquote>
			
			<p>An important property of inductors that will aid in the analysis of the transient response is that <alert>the current through an inductor cannot change instantaneously.</alert> That is: <m>i(0^-)=i(0^+)</m>. In an ideal analysis where inductors have no parasitic capacitance (which is what this textbook will assume), the voltage dropped over an inductor can change instantaneously and therefore be discontinuous in time.</p>

			<p>The procedure for analyzing the transient response of an RL circuit includes the following steps.</p>

			<p>
				<ol>
					<li><p>Calculate the initial current (<m>i(0)</m>) through the circuit element by analyzing the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. (If necessary, calculate at time <m>t=0^-</m> and at time <m>t=0^+</m>.)</p></li>
					<li><p>Calculate the final current (<m>i(\infty)</m>) through the circuit element by analyzing the circuit in its final steady-state condition.</p></li>
					<li><p>If there are no dependent sources: At time <m>t=0^+</m>, disconnect all independent sources and calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the inductance. Use this value to calculate the time constant of the circuit.</p></li>
					<li><p>If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit: Either calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit and use the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance as the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor or derive the differential equation for the circuit and use <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" /> to calculate the time constant of the circuit.</p></li>
					<li><p>Plug the above values into the equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p></li>
					<li><p>Use the equation relating voltage and current for the circuit element of interest to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p></li>
				</ol>
			</p>

			<p>All RL circuits can be analyzed in this method to derive a transient response. Before considering circuits of this type in <xref ref="ch6-generaltransient" />, we will first look at two special conditions of the transient response: the homogeneous transient response (i.e. <m>i(\infty) = 0</m>) and the charging from zero transient response (i.e. <m>i(0) = 0</m>).</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch6-discharging">
			<title>Discharging to Zero (Homogeneous) Transient Response</title>

			<p>The discharging to zero response is a special case of the transient response that specifically looks at what happens when an inductor is initially connected to a DC current that is suddenly removed from a circuit. In other words: the inductor, after being disconnected from a power supply, discharges its stored energy through the resistive element(s) of the circuit. The circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="rldischarging" /> will be used to analyze this discharging response. The switch is in position a for a long time before suddenly moving to position b at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="rldischarging">
  				<caption>An RL circuit used to analyze the discharging to zero (homogeneous) transient response.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-rldischarging">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with four nodes consisting of a current source Iin, inductor L, resistor R, and SPDT switch. Iin is connected to one output of the SPDT switch at node a. The current source Iin flows into node a. The resistor connects to the other output of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the inductor at node c. The current source Iin, the inductor, and the resistor all connect to ground at node d. The current source Iin flows out of node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage drop v(t) is defined over the inductor from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is depicted as moving from position a to position b.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2,1) node[cute spdt up arrow, rotate=90, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:2.5) node[ground] {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {b} to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l_=$R$] ++(down:3) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2.5)$);
							\draw[]	($(switch.in)-(0,2.5)$) to [short] ++(left:2.5) to [I, l^=$I_{IN}$] ++(up:3) |- (switch.out 1) node[above] {a};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws can be used to analyze the circuit in the initial condition. Because the only two elements are the current source and the inductor, and they are in series, the initial current through the inductor must be equal to the source current. In other words: <m>i(0) = I_{IN}</m>.</p>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws are also used to analyze the circuit in the discharging condition, once the switch has been flipped to position b. The voltage drop over the inductor (<m>v(t)</m>, defined in <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />) is equal to the voltage drop over the resistor. Because the two components are in series they share a current of <m>i(t)</m>. The discharging transient response is derived from these properties in <xref ref="ch6-eqdischargingrl" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch6-eqdischargingrlproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-eqdischargingrl">0 = \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R}{L}i(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-eqdischargingrlproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>0 \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R}{L}i(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>The equation for the current in this transient discharging analysis is a first-order, linear, ordinary differential equation. This is consistent with the fact that the circuit itself is a first-order, linear circuit! The solution to the differential equation will not be derived in this book but is given in <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" />. Note the similarities with the RC homogeneous solution.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent">i(t) = i(0^+)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t) + i(0^-)~u(-t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>Using <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />, the transient response of the voltage drop over the discharging circuit can also be calculated. This is shown in <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltageproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage">v(t) = -Ri(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltageproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(t) \amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(0^+)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t) + i(0^-)~u(-t)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(0^+) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}\right]~u(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(0^-)\right]~u(-t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\left(-\frac{R}{L}\right)i(0^+) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= -Ri(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>The quantity <m>\tau</m> is the time constant of the circuit. It defines how quickly the current and voltage change at any point in the circuit. Larger <m>\tau</m> values imply that the inductor will take a longer time to discharge. When comparing the homogeneous transient response given in <xref ref="ch6-eqdischargingrl" /> with the standard form of a differential equation shown in <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" />, we can find the value of <m>\tau</m>, given in <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" />, where <m>R</m> is the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor (assuming there are no dependent sources or op-amps present in the circuit). If there are dependent sources or op-amps in the circuit, the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit seen by the inductor can be used to determine this equivalent resistance. (Alternatively, the differential equation can be derived from the circuit without doing a Th&#233;venin equivalence, and <m>\tau</m> can be determined by analyzing the coefficient <m>a_0</m> of the equation in standard form.)</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-taurlequation">\tau = \frac{L}{R}</md>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Homogeneous transient response of an RL circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown below. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-dischargingrlexamplefig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an 8V source, 2.4k resistor, 6k resistor, 9k resistor, 0.3H inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 8V source and the 2.4k resistor connect at node a. The 2.4k resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, the 6k resistor, and the 9k resistor connect at node c. The 6k resistor and 0.3H inductor connect at node d. The 0.3H inductor, the 9k resistor, and the low-potential end of the 8V source connect at node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over the inductor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) is defined through the inductor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=8~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=2.4~k$\Omega$]						(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute opening switch]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=0.3~H, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]									(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l^=9~k$\Omega$]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>First, calculate the initial conditions. Re-draw the circuit to represent the time before the switch opens. Because the circuit is under DC conditions, the inductor can be represented as a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-dischargingrlexamplefig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 8V source, 2.4k resistor, 6k resistor, and 9k resistor. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 2.4k resistor at node a. The 2.4k, 6k, and 9k resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 6k and 9k resistors at node c. Branch current i(0) is defined through the 6k resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=8~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=2.4~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(0)$, i_=$i(0)$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]								(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=9~k$\Omega$]					(5,0);
									\draw[] 	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial voltage drop over the inductor will be zero. Source transformation can be used to calculate the initial current through the inductor. The current source will have a value of</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{8~\textrm{V}}{2.4~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 3.33~\textrm{mA}.</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-dischargingrlexamplefig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 3.33mA source, 2.4k resistor, 6k resistor, and 9k resistor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 3.33mA source flows out of node b and into node a. Branch current i(0) is defined through the 6k resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=3.33~mA]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.4~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, i_=$i(0)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=9~k$\Omega$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0^-) \amp= 3.33~\textrm{mA} \left(\frac{1.44~\textrm{k}\Omega}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.8~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the discharging circuit. Re-draw for time <m>t \geq ~0</m> (when the switch is open and the current source is disconnected from the circuit).</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-dischargingrlexamplefig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 6k resistor, 9k resistor, and 0.3H inductor. The 6k and 9k resistors connect at node a. The 6k resistor and 0.3H inductor connect at node b. The 0.3H inductor and 9k resistor connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=0.3~H, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]								(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=9~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance seen by the inductor is 15<nbsp />k&#8486;. This is because the two resistors are in series with the inductor. This equivalent resistance can be used to calculate the RL time constant using <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.3~\textrm{H}}{15~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2\times10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> can now be derived using <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" /> and <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.8~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-50000t}~u(t) + 0.8~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -12~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-50000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-dischargingrlexamplefig5">
							<shortdescription>Top: Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as a function of t in ms. The graph is at 0.8mA until a time of 0, then exponentially decays to 0mA. Bottom: graph of v(t) in V plotted on the same x-axis. The graph is 0 until 0+, where it jumps to (0,-12), then exponentially decays to 0.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-0.02, xmax=0.08]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0.8) (0,0.8)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:0.4, samples=50] {0.8*exp(-50*x)};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$v(t)$~(V)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-1,0) (0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,-12)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:0.4, samples=50] {-12*exp(-50*x)};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch6-charging">
			<title>Charging from Zero Transient Response</title>

			<p>The charging from zero transient response of a circuit corresponds to how the voltage and/or current changes with respect to a sudden application of power to the circuit components. This is another specific case of the general RC transient response, which will be discussed in <xref ref="ch6-generaltransient" />. This section specifically corresponds to scenarios where a fully discharged inductor is suddenly connected to a circuit containing at least one resistive element and a power supply. The circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="nonhomogeneousrlcircuitfigure" /> will be used to analyze the charging response. The switch is in position b for a long time before suddenly moving to position a at time <m>t=0</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="nonhomogeneousrlcircuitfigure">
  				<caption>An RL circuit used to analyze the charging from zero transient response.</caption>
				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-nonhomogeneousrlcircuitfigure">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes consisting of a current source Iin, inductor L, resistor R, resistor RD, and SPDT switch. Iin is connected to R and output a of the SPDT switch at node a. Iin flows into node a. RD connects to the other output of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the inductor at node c. Iin, the inductor, and RD all connect to ground at node d. Iin flows out of node d. Branch current i(t) is defined through the inductor from node c to node d. Voltage drop v(t) is defined over the inductor from node c to node d. The SPDT switch is depicted as moving from position b to position a.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(2,1) node[cute spdt down arrow, rotate=90, anchor=in] (switch) {};
							\draw[]	($(switch.out 1)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=$R$, *-*] ++(down:3.2) -- ($(switch.in)-(2.5,2.5)$);
							\draw[]	(switch.in) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*] ++(down:2.5) node[ground] {};
							\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [short] ++(left:2.5);
							\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {b} to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [R, l_=$R_D$] ++(down:3) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2.5)$);
							\draw[]	($(switch.in)-(0,2.5)$) to [short]	++(left:5) to [I, l^=$I_{IN}$] ++(up:3) |- ($(switch.out 1)-(2.5,0)$);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>In the initial state of the circuit, all of the energy that may have been stored in the inductor has completely discharged through the discharging resistor (<m>R_D</m>) resulting in an initial current of 0<nbsp />mA.</p>

			<p>In the charging condition (when the switch has been moved to position a), Kirchhoff's current law can be used to analyze the current through each circuit element. From this, the differential equation for <m>i(t)</m> can be derived. This solution is expressed in <xref ref="ch6-chargingrldiffeq" />, and the derivation is shown in <xref ref="ch6-chargingrldiffeqproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingrldiffeq">\frac{R}{L}I_{IN} = \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R}{L}i(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-chargingrldiffeqproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>I_{IN} \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R} + i(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>v(t) \amp= L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
						<mrow>I_{IN} \amp= \frac{L}{R}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{R}{L}I_{IN} \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R}{L}i(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>The solution to this differential equation (assuming that the initial current through the inductor is 0<nbsp />mA) is given in <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" />. Again, note the similarities with the RC circuit charging transient response.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingrlcurrent">i(t) = i(\infty)~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}\right]~u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>Similar to the homogeneous RL circuit, the time constant <m>\tau</m> defines how quickly (or slowly) the inductor responds to changes in current. The equation for the time constant continues to be defined by <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> and is calculated in the same manner as with homogeneous RL circuits.</p>

			<p><xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> can be used to calculate the voltage drop over the inductor during the transient charging process. The result is expressed in <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltage" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltageproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingrlvoltage">Ri(\infty)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-chargingrlvoltageproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(t) \amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)~\left(1-\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}\right)~u(t)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)~u(t)-i(\infty)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)\right]~u(t) - L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= -L\left(-\frac{R}{L}\right)i(\infty)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= Ri(\infty)~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<example>
				<title>Charging from zero transient response</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample1fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a 6mA source, three resistors (two 10ohm and one 12ohm), a 50mH inductor, and an SPST switch. The 6mA source connects to both 10ohm resistors at node a. The 6mA source flows into node a. The 10ohm (B) connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 12ohm and 50mH at node c. The 6mA source, 10ohm (A), 50mH, and 12ohm connect at node d. The 6mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]								(0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]					(7.5,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=50~mH, *-*, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]						(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The initial voltage and current properties of the inductor are zero. This is because the inductor is disconnected from the source in the initial steady-state, and any current has long since discharged through the 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<p>Calculate the final (steady-state) conditions of the circuit. Re-draw the circuit to represent the time well after the switch closes. Because the circuit is under DC conditions, the inductor can be represented as a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample1fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 6mA source, a short, two 10ohm resistors, and a 12ohm resistor. The 6mA source connects to both 10ohm resistors at node a. The 6mA source flows into node a. The 10ohm resistor (B), one end of the short, and the 12ohm connect at node b. The 6mA source, 12ohm, 10ohm (A), and the other end of the short connect at node c. The 6mA source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) is defined over the short from node b to node c. Branch current i(infinity) is defined through the short from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]							(0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-*, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is shorted out of the circuit by the inductor. Re-draw the circuit with that resistor removed.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample1fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 6mA source and two 10ohm resistors. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 6mA source flows from node a to node b. Branch current i(infinity) flows through one of the 10ohm resistors from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]							(0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. The equivalent resistance of the circuit is 5<nbsp />&#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= 6~\textrm{mA} \left(\frac{5~\Omega}{10~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the charging circuit. Re-draw for time <m>t \geq 0</m>, which is when the switch is closed. Deactivate the current source by replacing it with an open to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample1fig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of two 10ohm resistors, a 12ohm resistor, and a 50mH inductor. The two 10ohm resistors connect at node a. The 10ohm (B), 50mH, and 12ohm connect at node b. The 10ohm (A), 50mH, and 12ohm connect at node c. Voltage v(t) is defined over the inductor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=50~mH, *-*, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is calculated below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (10~\Omega+10~\Omega)//12~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\Omega//12~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.5~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate the RL time constant.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.05~\textrm{H}}{7.5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 0.0067~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> can now be derived using <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> and <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltage" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 3~\textrm{mA}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-150t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 22.5~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-150t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>These two signals are plotted below as functions of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample1fig5">
							<shortdescription>Top: Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as a function of t in ms. The graph is at 0mA until a time of 0, then exponentially decays to 3mA. Bottom: graph of v(t) in V plotted on the same x-axis. The graph is 0 until 0+, where it jumps to (0,22.5), then exponentially decays to 0.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{groupplot}[group style={group name=my plots, group size=1 by 2, vertical sep=0pt, xlabels at=edge bottom, xticklabels at=edge bottom,}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xlabel=$t$~(ms), xmin=-7, xmax=28]
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-7,0) (0,0)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:50, samples=50] {3-3*exp(-0.15*x)};
										\nextgroupplot[ylabel=$v(t)$~(mV)]
											\addplot[thick] coordinates {(-7,0) (0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
											\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,22.5)};
											\addplot[thick, domain=0:50, samples=50] {22.5*exp(-0.15*x)};
									\end{groupplot}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

			<example xml:id="ch6-charging-from-zero-example-with-dependent-source">
				<title>Charging from zero transient response with a dependent source</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown below. Assume that the inductor is completely discharged in the initial steady-state condition. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds. The parameter values are <m>I_S = 2~\textrm{A}</m>, <m>R_1 = 2~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 3~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 25~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>k = 0.1</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a current source Is, resistors R1 and R2, VCVS k*Vx, inductor L, and SPST switch. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 connects to one end of the SPST switch at node c. The other end of the SPST switch connects to L at node d. Is, R1, and L connect at node e. Is flows out of node e. Voltage Vx is defined over R1 from node a to node e. Voltage v(t) is defined over L from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) is defined through L and flows from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]				(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, v^=$V_X$, *-*] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]	(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]				(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. In this condition, the switch has been closed for a long time. The inductor can be replaced by a short. Numeric quantities for each component will be used in this circuit diagram.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2A source, 2ohm resistor, 3ohm resistor, and 0.1*Vx VCVS. The 2A source, 2ohm, and high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. The 2A source flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to the 3ohm at node b. The 3ohm, 2ohm, and 2A source connect at node c. The 2A source flows out of node c. Vx drops over the 2ohm resistor from node a to node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 3ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=2~A]		(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]			(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$, *-*] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$0.1V_X$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=3~$\Omega$]	(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]				(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transformation can be used in this circuit, but it is not terribly intuitive, as it changes the position of <m>V_X</m> in the circuit. Instead, KCL and KVL will be used.</p>

					<p>First, use KCL at the node connecting all three circuit branches.</p>

					<p>
						<md>2~\textrm{A} = \frac{V_X}{2~\Omega} + i(\infty)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Then, use KCL around the right mesh of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= 0.1V_X + i(\infty)(3~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X - 0.1V_X \amp= i(\infty)(3~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>0.9V_X \amp= i(\infty)(3~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{0.9V_X}{3~\Omega} \amp= i(\infty)</mrow>
							<mrow>(0.3~\textrm{1/}\Omega)V_X - i(\infty) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>
						<alert>Method 1: Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit</alert>
					</p>

					<p>There are two equations and two unknowns. These two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>V_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>i(\infty)</m>, and the right column contains constants. The units of V, A, and &#8486; are used.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.3 	\amp -1	\amp 0\\
								0.5		\amp 1	\amp 2
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 2.5\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0.75\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that <m>i(\infty) = 0.75~\textrm{A}</m>.</p>

					<p>There are two methods that can be used to calculate <m>\tau</m>. In either case, the circuit should be analyzed at <m>t = 0^+</m>, so the switch will be closed. The first method that will be used in this example is to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor and use that Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance in <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" />.</p>

					<p>The Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of current source Is, resistors R1 and R2, and k*Vx VCVS. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 connects at node c. Is and R1 connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is measured from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]		(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]			(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, v^=$V_X$, *-*] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, -o]	(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]		(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the test source method to derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance. The independent current source has been deactivated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2fig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and one mesh consisting of voltage source Vtest, VCVS k*Vx, and resistors R1 and R2. R1 and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 connects to the high-potential end of Vtest at node c. The low-potential end of Vtest connects to R1 at node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Branch current Itest is defined as flowing through Vtest from node do to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, v^=$V_X$]		 ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{TEST}$, i^=$I_{TEST}$]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law over <m>R_1</m> to derive an equation for <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq1">V_X = R_1I_{TEST}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL around the circuit's single loop to derive an equation for <m>V_{TEST}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md  number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq2">V_{TEST} = R_2I_{TEST} - kV_X + R_1I_{TEST}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq1" /> into <xref ref="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq2" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{TEST} \amp= R_2I_{TEST} - kR_1I_{TEST} + R_1I_{TEST}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= R_2I_{TEST} + (1-k)R_1I_{TEST}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TEST}}{I_{TEST}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_2I_{TEST} + (1-k)R_1I_{TEST}}{I_{TEST}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= R_2 + (1-k)R_1</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\Omega + (1-0.1)(2~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\Omega + (0.9)(2~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\Omega + 1.8~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.8~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{25 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{H}}{4.8~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0052~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>
						<alert>Method 2: Deriving the differential equation</alert>
					</p>

					<p>The second method that will be used is to derive the differential equation and use <xref ref="ch5-firstorderlinearode-standard" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2fig5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of current source Is, VCVS k*Vx, resistors R1 and R2, and inductor L. Is, R1, and the high-potential end of the VCVS connect at node a. Is flows into node a. The low-potential end of the VCVS connects to R2 at node b. R2 and L connect at node c. Is, R1, and L connect at node d. Is flows out of node d. Voltage Vx drops over R1 from node a to node d. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]				(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, v^=$V_X$, *-*] ++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$kV_X$]	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL around the right loop of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq3">V_X = kV_X + R_2i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting the three circuit branches.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq4">I_S = \frac{V_X}{R_1} + i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve <xref ref="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq4" /> for <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq5">V_X = R_1I_S - R_1i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq5" /> into <xref ref="ch6-chargingfromzeroexample2eq3" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_1I_S - R_1i(t) \amp= k(R_1I_S - R_1i(t)) + R_2i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_1I_S - kR_1I_S \amp= R_1i(t) - kR_1i(t) + R_2i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>(1-k)R_1I_S \amp= (1-k)R_1i(t) + R_2i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>(1-k)R_1I_S \amp= \left((1-k)R_1 + R_2\right)i(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{(1-k)R_1I_S}{L} \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{(1-k)R_1+R_2}{L}i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Numeric quantities for each parameter can now be plugged in to calculate the differential equation. Units are not included in the equation, but base units of A, &#8486;, and H are used.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{(1-0.1)(2)(2)}{25 \times 10^{-3}} \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{(1-0.1)(2)+3}{25 \times 10^{-3}}i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>3.6 \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + 192 i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This yields the same result as the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit method, as expected. If we had not already derived the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, we could use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to solve for <m>R</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{L}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{192}~\textrm{s} \amp= \frac{25 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{H}}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>R \amp= (25 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{H})(192~\textrm{s}^{-1})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.8~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m> and <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.75~\textrm{A}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-192t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 3.6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-192t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
				
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch6-generaltransient">
			<title>General Transient Response</title>

			<p>Two special cases of the RL transient response have been analyzed: the homogeneous response (<m>i(\infty)=0</m>) and the charging from zero response (<m>i(0)=0</m>). In general, the initial and final currents through an inductor can be any arbitrary values. The most general form of the equation for the transient response of an RL circuit is therefore given in <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-generalrlcurrent">i(t) = \left[i(\infty) + \left( i(0^+) - i(\infty) \right) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}} \right]~u(t) + i(0^-)~u(-t)</md>
			</p>

			<p>The time constant <m>\tau</m> defines how quickly (or slowly) the inductor responds to changes in current. The equation for the time constant continues to be defined by <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> and is calculated in the same manner as with previously defined RL circuits.</p>

			<p>Voltage dropped over the inductor can be calculated by using <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. The solution is shown in <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltage" /> and derived in <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltageproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-generalrlvoltage">v(t) = R \left[i(\infty) - i(0^+)\right] \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="ch6-generalrlvoltageproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>v(t) \amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[\left[i(\infty) + \left( i(0^+) - i(\infty) \right) \textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}} \right]~u(t) + i(0^-)~u(-t)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)~u(t) + i(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t) - i(\infty)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t) + i(0^-)u(-t)\right]</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)\right]~u(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}\right]~u(t) - L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(\infty)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}\right]~u(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}\left[i(0^-)\right]~u(-t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= L\left(-\frac{R}{L}\right)i(0^+)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t) - L\left(-\frac{R}{L}\right)i(\infty)\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp= R\left[i(\infty)-i(0^+)\right]~\textrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{L/R}}~u(t)</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>All of the equations in this section of the text have discussed calculating the current through the inductor. It is also possible to derive a first-order equation to calculate voltage over or current through any arbitrary component in a circuit. It is important to note that the initial conditions may be discontinuous. This is because while the current through an inductor is continuous for all time, that is not necessarily true for other circuit elements such as resistors, particularly if they are not connected in series with an inductor.</p>

			<p>To calculate the transient <alert>current</alert> response through a circuit element with a discontinuous current, <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> can still be used. Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state to calculate <m>i(0^-)</m>. Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. Analyze the circuit at the exact moment the conditions change to calculate <m>i(0^+)</m>, using the current continuity of the inductor to help calculate any necessary quantities.</p>

			<p>To calculate the transient <alert>voltage</alert> response over a circuit element, use the appropriate relationship between voltage and current through that circuit element. (For example, Ohm's law will be used to calculate the voltage dropped over a resistor.)</p>

			<p>Calculation of the time constant is unaffected by the choice of circuit element.</p>

			<example>
				<title>General transient response of an inductor</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> for the circuit below. Switch S1 is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds. Switch S2 is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0.005<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of an 8V source, 12V source, four 12ohm resistors (R1, R2, R3, and R4), a 20mH inductor, and two SPST switches (S1 and S2). The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to R1 at node a. R1 connects to one end of S1 at node b. The other end of S1 connects to R2, S2, and the 20mH inductor at node c. The other end of S2, R3, and R4 connect at node d. R4 connects to the high-potential end of the 12V source at node e. The low-potential end of both sources, R2, R3, and the 20mH inductor connect at node f. Branch current i(t) is defined as flowing through the 20mH inductor from node c to node f. Switch S1 is depicted as opening. Switch S2 is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=8~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]				++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [cute opening switch, l^=S1]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=20~mH, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [cute closing switch, l^=S2]	++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]			++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,\H) to [V, l_=12~V]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							++(right:5*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the initial conditions of the circuit. Switch S1 has been closed for a long time and switch S2 is open. The inductor can be replaced with a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 8V source, two 12ohm resistors (R1 and R2) and a short. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the short connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 8V source, R2, and the short connect at node c. Branch current i(0) is defined as flowing through the short from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=8~V]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]			++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistor in parallel with the inductor (which acts like a short) is shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of an 8V source and 12ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 12ohm resistor at node a. The 12ohm resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 8V source at node b. Branch current i(0) flows through the 12ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=8~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]	++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]				++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{12~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.667~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 667~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means the equation for <m>i(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>667~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</m>, representing the initial steady-state conditions of the circuit. This creates an <m>i(t)</m> graph that so far appears as follows.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig4">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(mA)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=0, ymax=1250, ytick={0,250,...,1250}]
											\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,667) (0,667)};
	    								\end{axis}
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with both switches open to determine how the circuit will act between 0<nbsp />s and 0.005<nbsp />s (5<nbsp />ms). Both sources are disconnected from the circuit in this interval of time.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 12ohm resistor and 20mH inductor. The two circuit elements connect on both ends at each node. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=20~mH, i_=$i(t)$]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]						++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance seen by the inductor is 12<nbsp />&#8486; during this time interval. Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m> during this interval of time.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{20 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{H}}{12~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.67 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that the equation for <m>i(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>667~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-600t}~u(t)</m>. The graph of the current containing both terms appears below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig6">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667). Then the current exponentially decays to zero.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(mA)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=0, ymax=1250, ytick={0,250,...,1250}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,667) (0,667)};
										\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:25, samples=50] {667*e^(-0.6*x)};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Before analyzing the circuit when S2 closes, calculate the value of <m>i(t)</m> at 5<nbsp />ms, which represents the <q>initial condition</q> experienced by the inductor when that switch closes.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0.005) \amp= 667~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-600(0.005)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 667~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-3}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (667~\textrm{mA})(0.0498)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 33.2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>At 0.005<nbsp />s, the exponential term describing the decay with <m>\tau = 1.67 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{s}</m> no longer dictates how the circuit operates. This term must be subtracted out at <m>t = 0.005~\textrm{s}</m>. Therefore, the equation for <m>i(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>-33.2~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-600(t-0.005)}~u(t-0.005)</m>. The graph of the current containing all three terms is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig7">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667). Then the current exponentially decays to (5,33.2).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(mA)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=0, ymax=1250, ytick={0,250,...,1250}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,667) (0,667)};
										\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:5, samples=50] {667*e^(-0.6*x)};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0.005^+~\textrm{s}</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the inductor to calculate the value of <m>\tau</m> in this condition. The 12<nbsp />V source has been deactivated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three 12ohm resistors and a 20mH inductor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=20~mH, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]			++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,\H) to [short]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]							++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The three 12<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are in parallel. Therefore, the equivalent resistance is one-third of 12<nbsp />&#8486;, which is 4<nbsp />&#8486;. Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate the value of <m>\tau</m> for the circuit after 0.005<nbsp />s.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{20 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{H}}{4~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. Switch S1 is open and S2 has been closed for a very long time. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig9">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three 12ohm resistors (R1, R2, and R3), a short, and a 12V source. R1, R2, R3, and the short all connect at node a. R3 and the high-potential end of the 12V source connect at node b. R1, R2, the short, and the low-potential end of the 12V source connect at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short]						++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]			++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,\H) to [V, l_=12~V]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]							++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistors in parallel with the inductor (which acts like a short) are shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig10">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 12ohm resistor and 12V source. The 12ohm resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 12V source at node a. The high-potential end of the 12V source connects to the 12ohm resistor at node b. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 12ohm resistor from node b to node a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [V, l_=12~V]				++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,0) to [short]						++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{12~\textrm{V}}{12~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1000~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means the equation for <m>i(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>\left[1000~\textrm{mA} - 966.8~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-200(t-0.005)}\right]~u(t)</m>. The graph of <m>i(t)</m> including all terms is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample1fig11">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is a straight line from (-5,667) to (0,667). Then the current exponentially decays to (5,33.2). Then the current exponentially decays to 1000 with a different time-constant.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(mA)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=0, ymax=1250, ytick={0,250,...,1250}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,667) (0,667)};
										\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:5, samples=50] {667*e^(-0.6*x)};
										\addplot[black, thick, domain=5:25, samples=50] {1000-966.8*e^(-0.2*(x-5))};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The overall equation for <m>i(t)</m> is expressed below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 667~\textrm{mA}~u(-t) + 667~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-600t}~u(t) - 33.2~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-600(t-0.005)}~u(t-0.005) + \left[1000~\textrm{mA} - 966.8~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-200(t-0.005)}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
					
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>General transient response of a resistor in an RL circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit below. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of 0<nbsp />seconds. The switch then re-opens at a time of 0.01<nbsp />seconds.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 30V source, 10mA source, 12ohm resistor, 36ohm resistor, 6ohm resistor, 30ohm resistor, 5ohm resistor, 100mH inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm at node a. The 12ohm connects to the SPST at node b. The other end of the SPST connects to the 36ohm and 6ohm resistors at node c. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node d. The 5ohm resistor, 100mH inductor, and 10mA source connect at node e. The 10mA source flows into node e. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 36ohm resistor, 30ohm resistor, 100mH inductor, and 10mA source connect at node f. The 10mA source flows out of node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node d to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]										++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [cute closing switch]							++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [L, l_=100~mH, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]									++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											++(right:5*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the initial conditions of the circuit before the switch closes at 0<nbsp />seconds. At this time, the switch is open and the inductor acts as a short. The 30<nbsp />V source is disconnected from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, 5ohm resistors, a short, and a 10mA source. The 36ohm and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor connects to the short and 10mA source at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. The 36ohm and 30ohm resistors connect to the short and 10mA source at node d. The 10mA source flow out of node d. Voltage v(0-) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(0-) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current iL(0) flows through the short from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$]								++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^-)$, i_=$i(0^-)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short, i_=$i_L(0)$, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short]											++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]										++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]												++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All of the resistors are shorted out of the circuit. Therefore, <m>i(0^-)</m> is equal to 0. However, the inductor will have an initial current equal to the source current: 10<nbsp />mA. This is important to know because the inductor current is continuous with time.</p>

					<p>To calculate <m>i(0^+)</m>, analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m>. Treat the inductor as a source of 10<nbsp />mA. The switch is closed.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors, and two 10mA sources. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm at node a. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The 5ohm resistor connects to both 10mA sources at node d. One 10mA source flows into node d and the other 10mA source flows out of node d. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 36ohm, 30ohm, and both 10mA sources connect at node e. One of the 10mA sources flows into node e, and the other flows out of node e. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]											++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]								++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]									++(right:1.5*\H);
									\draw[]	(3.5*\H,\H) to [I, l_=10~mA, *-*]								++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3.5*\H,\H) to [short]											++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4.5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]										++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												++(right:4.5*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources can be combined together into a single current source of 0<nbsp />mA. This is equivalent to a branch with no current, which can be represented as an open circuit. (No current flows through the 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm at node a. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The other end of the 5ohm resistor dangles (connects to an open). The low-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 36ohm and 30ohm resistors at node d. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]											++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]								++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor does not contribute to the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, and 30ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm at node a. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm and 30ohm resistors connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 36ohm and 30ohm resistors at node d. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]										++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Apply the voltage divider rule to the two-mesh circuit to calculate <m>v(0^+)</m>. The equivalent resistance of the right-hand mesh is equal to</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 36~\Omega // (6~\Omega + 30~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 36~\Omega // 36~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\Omega.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(0^+) \amp= 30~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{18~\Omega}{12~\Omega+18~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{30~\Omega}{30~\Omega+6~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{18~\Omega}{30~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{30~\Omega}{36~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 30~\textrm{V}(0.6)(0.833)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0^+)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0^+) \amp= \frac{v(0^+)}{30~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{15~\textrm{V}}{30~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.5~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with the switch closed to determine how the switch will act between 0<nbsp />s and 0.01<nbsp />s.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 10mA source, 100mH inductor, and 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm at node a. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The 5ohm resistor, 100mH inductor, and 10mA source connect at node d. The 10mA source flows into node d. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 36ohm resistor, 30ohm resistor, 100mH inductor, and 10mA source connect at node e. The 10mA source flows out of node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]										++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=100~mH, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]									++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor can be calculated by disconnecting all independent sources.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors and 100mH inductor. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor connects to the 100mH inductor at node c. The 12ohm, 36ohm, 30ohm resistors and 100mH inductor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short]											++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=100~mH]									++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is equal to</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (((12~\Omega // 36~\Omega) + 6~\Omega) // 30~\Omega) + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= ((9~\Omega + 6~\Omega) // 30~\Omega) + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (15~\Omega // 30~\Omega) + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10~\Omega + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15~\Omega.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.1~\textrm{H}}{15~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.00667~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the final steady-state value of the current if the switch were to remain closed. This value will be referred to as <m>i(\infty_c)</m>, where the subscript c means closed. The inductor can be replaced by a short. The current through the inductor will also need to be calculated, as it represents a continuous quantity that can be used when we need to calculate the current through the resistor at the instant the switch re-opens.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 10mA source, 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors, and a short. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm resistor at node a. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The 5ohm resistor, short, and 10mA source connect at node d. The 10mA source flows into node d. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 36ohm and 30ohm resistors, short, and 10mA source connect at node e. The 10mA source flows out of node e. Voltage v(infinity,c) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current i(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current iL(infinity,c) flows through the short from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]												++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]									++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty_c)$, i_=$i(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_L(\infty_c)$, *-*]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short]												++(right:1.5*\H);
									\draw[]	(4.5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]											++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													++(right:4.5*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Superposition will be the simplest method to solve for both <m>i(\infty_c)</m> and <m>i_L(\infty_c)</m>. Start by disconnecting the current source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig8b">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 12ohm resistor at node a. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 36ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors at node d. Voltage v1(infinity,c) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i1(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current iL1(infinity,c) flows through the 5ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=30~V]												++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]									++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v_1(\infty_c)$, i_=$i_1(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L1}(\infty_c)$]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]													++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{30~\textrm{V}}{12~\Omega} = 2.5~\textrm{A}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig9">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 2.5A source, and 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 2.5A source, 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 2.5A source flows into node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 2.5A source and 12ohm, 36ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The 2.5A source flows out of node c. Branch current i1(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current iL1(infinity,c) flows through the 5ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [I, l^=2.5~A]							++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i_1(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L1}(\infty_c)$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [short]									++(right:4*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig10">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 2.5A source and 9ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 2.5A source connects to the 9ohm and 6ohm resistors at node a. The 2.5A source flows into node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 2.5A source connects to the 9ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors at node c. The 2.5A source flows out of node c. Branch current i1(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current iL1(infinity,c) flows through the 5ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [I, l^=2.5~A]							++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=9~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i_1(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L1}(\infty_c)$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [short]									++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the current source to a voltage source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>(2.5~\textrm{A})(9~\Omega) = 22.5~\textrm{V}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig11">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 22.5V source and 9ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 22.5V source connects to the 9ohm at node a. The 9ohm and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node c. The low-potential end of the 22.5V source connects to the 30ohm and 5ohm resistors at node d. Branch current i1(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current iL1(infinity,c) flows through the 5ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [V, l_=22.5~V]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [R, l^=9~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i_1(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L1}(\infty_c)$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [short]									++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig12">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 22.5V source and 15ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The high-potential end of the 22.5V source connects to the 15ohm resistor at node a. The 15ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 22.5V source connects to the 30ohm and 5ohm resistors at node c. Branch current i1(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current iL1(infinity,c) flows through the 5ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [V, l_=22.5~V]									++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=15~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i_1(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L1}(\infty_c)$]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Transform the voltage source into a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{22.5~\textrm{V}}{15~\Omega} = 1.5~\textrm{A}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig13">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 1.5A source and 15ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 1.5A source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i1(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current iL1(infinity,c) flows through the 5ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [I, l^=1.5~A]							++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l_=15~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i_1(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]						++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L1}(\infty_c)$]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(-\H,0) to [short]									++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{15~\Omega} + \frac{1}{30~\Omega} + \frac{1}{5~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.33~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i_1(\infty_c)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_1(\infty_c) \amp= 1.5~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{3.33~\Omega}{30~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.167~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i_{L1}(\infty_c)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_{L1}(\infty_c) \amp= 1.5~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{3.33~\Omega}{5~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit with the voltage source deactivated.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig14">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 10mA source, a short, and 12ohm, 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 12ohm, 36ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor connects to the short and the 10mA source at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. The 12ohm, 36ohm, 30ohm resistors, short, and 10mA source connect at node d. The 10mA source flows out of node d. Branch current i2(infinity,c) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current iL2(infinity,c) flows through the short from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [short]											++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [R, l^=12~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i_2(\infty_c)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short, i^=$i_{L2}(\infty_c)$, *-*]			++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short]										++(right:1.5*\H);
									\draw[]	(4.5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]									++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											++(right:4.5*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All of the resistors are shorted out of the circuit and will have no current. This means that <m>i_2(\infty_c)</m> is zero. The total current through the 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is 0.167<nbsp />A.</p>

					<p>All of the current from the source will flow through the inductor (which acts like a short). Calculate the total value of <m>i_L(\infty_c)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_L(\infty_c) \amp= 1~\textrm{A} + 0.01~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.01~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>This means the equation for <m>i(t)</m> will have a term of <m>\left[0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.333~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-150t}\right]~u(t)</m>. The graph of the current so far is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig15">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is zero from -5ms to 0-. At 0+, the current becomes 0.5mA. Then the current exponentially decays to 0.167mA.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(A)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=-1, ymax=1, ytick={-1,-0.75,...,1}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0.5)};												\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:25, samples=50] {0.167+0.333*e^(-0.15*x)};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Before analyzing the circuit when the switch re-opens, calculate the value of <m>i(t)</m> at a time of 0.01^-<nbsp />s. This will act as the value to subtract out of the equation for the second time interval.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0.01^-) \amp= 0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.333~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-150(0.01)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp=  0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.333~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-1.5}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.333~\textrm{A}(0.223)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.074~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.241~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The equation for <m>i(t)</m> will contain a term of <m>\left[-0.167~\textrm{A}-0.074~\textrm{e}^{-150(t-0.01)}\right]~u(t-0.01)</m>. The graph of <m>i(t)</m> so far is plotted below. (Note the open circle at 0.01<nbsp />s; this is due to the discontinuity in current through a resistor.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig16">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is zero from -5 to 0-. The current becomes 0.5mA at t=0+. Then the current exponentially decays to (10-,0.241). Then the current exponentially decays to -2V with a different time-constant.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(A)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=-1, ymax=1, ytick={-1,-0.75,...,1}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0.5)};												\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:10, samples=50] {0.167+0.333*e^(-0.15*x)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(10,0.241)};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0.01^+</m> to calculate the new <q>initial condition</q> of the current at that time. The switch has re-opened, and the inductor can be replaced by a current source of 1.01<nbsp />A, as that was the value of the current at a time of 0.01<nbsp />s.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig17">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 1.01A source, 10mA source, and 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 36ohm and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor connects to both current sources at node c. The 1.01A source flows out of node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. The 30ohm and 30ohm resistors connect to both sources at node d. The 1.01A source flows into node d. The 10mA source flows out of node d. Branch current i(0.01+) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]							++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i(0.01^+)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]							++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [I, l_=1.01~A, *-*]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]								++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]										++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two current sources and combine the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig18">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 1A source, and 42ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 42ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node a. The 5ohm resistor connects to the 1A source at node b. The 1A source flows out of node b. The 42ohm and 30ohm resistors connect to the 1A source at node c. The 1A source flows into node c. Branch current i(0.01+) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=42~$\Omega$]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short]									++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, i_=$i(0.01^+)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]							++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [I, l_=1~A]								++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]										++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 5<nbsp />&#8486; resistor does not affect the current through the 42<nbsp />&#8486; or 30<nbsp />&#8486; branches. The equivalent resistance of the circuit is therefore</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(42~\Omega)(30~\Omega)}{42~\Omega + 30~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1260~\Omega^2}{72~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 17.5~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0.01^+).</m> Note the negative sign on the source, which is due to the polarity of the current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0.01^+) \amp= -1~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{17.5~\Omega}{30~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -1~\textrm{A}(0.583)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.583~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0.01^+</m> to calculate the time constant of the circuit in the configuration with the switch open.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig19">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 10mA source, 100mH inductor, and 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 36ohm and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor, 100mH inductor, and 10mA source connect at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. The 36ohm and 30ohm resistors connect to the 100mH inductor and 10mA source at node d. The 10mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [L, l_=100~mH, *-*]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short]										++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]									++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]											++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Deactivate the current source to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig20">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 100mH inductor and 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 36ohm and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor and 100mH inductor connect at node c. The 36ohm and 30ohm resistors connect to the 100mH inductor at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$]							++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]								++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [L, l_=100~mH]									++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]											++(right:2*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is equal to</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= ((36~\Omega + 6~\Omega) // 30~\Omega) + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (42~\Omega // 30~\Omega) + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 17.5~\Omega + 5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 22.5~\Omega.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.1~\textrm{H}}{22.5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0044~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the final steady-state condition of the circuit with the switch re-opened. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig21">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 10mA source, a short, and 36ohm, 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors. The 36ohm and 6ohm resistors connect at node a. The 6ohm, 30ohm, and 5ohm resistors connect at node b. The 5ohm resistor connects to the short and 10mA source at node c. The 10mA source flows into node c. The 36ohm and 30ohm resistors connect to the short and 10mA source at node d. The 10mA source flows out of node d. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 30ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l_=36~$\Omega$]				++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [R, l^=5~$\Omega$]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short, *-*]						++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [short]							++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(5*\H,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]						++(up:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [short]								++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The resistors are shorted out of the circuit and have no current. Therefore, <m>i(\infty)</m> is equal to zero. The equation <m>i(t)</m> will therefore contain a term of <m>-0.583~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-225(t-0.01)}~u(t-0.01)</m>. The graph of current is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig22">
							<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is zero from -5 to 0-. The current becomes 0.5mA at t=0+. Then the current exponentially decays to (10-,0.241). The current becomes -0.583mA at t=10+. Then the current exponentially decays to zero with a different time-constant.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(A)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=-1, ymax=1, ytick={-1,-0.75,...,1}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0.5)};												\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:10, samples=50] {0.167+0.333*e^(-0.15*x)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(10,0.241)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(10,-0.583)};												\addplot[black, thick, domain=10:25, samples=50] {-0.583*e^(-0.225*(x-10))};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The overall equation for current is described below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.333~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-150t}\right]~u(t) + \left[-0.167~\textrm{A}-0.074~\textrm{e}^{-150(t-0.01)}\right]~u(t-0.01)-0.583~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-225(t-0.01)}~u(t-0.01)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(t).</m></p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= Ri(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (30~\Omega)\left(\left[0.167~\textrm{A} + 0.333~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-150t}\right]~u(t) + \left[-0.167~\textrm{A}-0.074~\textrm{e}^{-150(t-0.01)}\right]~u(t-0.01)-0.583~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-225(t-0.01)}~u(t-0.01)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left[5~\textrm{V} + 10~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-150t}\right]~u(t) + \left[-5~\textrm{V} - 2.23~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-150(t-0.01)}\right]~u(t-0.01) - 17.5~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-225(t-0.01)}~u(t-0.01)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The graph of voltage is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalexample2fig23">
							<shortdescription>Graph of v(t) in V plotted as function of time in ms. The graph is zero from -5 to 0-. The voltage becomes 15V at t=0+. Then the voltage exponentially decays to (10-,7.23). The voltage becomes -16.14V at t=10+. Then the voltage exponentially decays to zero with a different time-constant.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$v(t)$~(V)}, xlabel={$t$~(ms)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-5, xmax=25, ymin=-20, ymax=20, ytick={-20,-10,...,20}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-5,0) (0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(0,0)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(0,15)};												\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:10, samples=50] {5+10*e^(-0.15*x)};
										\addplot[only marks,fill=white,mark=*]coordinates{(10,7.23)};
										\addplot[only marks,mark=*]coordinates{(10,-17.5)};												\addplot[black, thick, domain=10:25, samples=50] {-17.5*e^(-0.225*(x-10))};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
				</statement>
			</example>
		</subsection>

	</section>

	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

    	<subexercises>
			<title>Equivalent Inductance</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch6-equivinduct-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent inductance of the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentinductance-example1-diagram" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentinductance-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-equivinduct-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of six inductors. The 10mH connects at node a. The 10mH, 30mH, and 20mH connect at node b. The 20mH and 50mH connect at node c. The 50mH and 60mH connect at node d. The 30mH, 40mH, and 60mH connect at node e. The 40mH connects at node f. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=10~mH, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=30~mH, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=20~mH]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=50~mH]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [L, l_=40~mH, o-]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [L, l_=60~mH]		(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>L_{EQ} = 74.4~\textrm{mH}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by combining the 20<nbsp />mH, 50<nbsp />mH, and 60<nbsp />mH inductors in series.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example1-solution1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of four inductors. The 10mH connects at node a. The 10mH, 30mH, and 130mH connect at node b. The 40mH, 30mH, and 130mH connect at node c. The 40mH connects at node d. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=10~mH, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=30~mH, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=130~mH]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [L, l_=40~mH, o-]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]				(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the 30<nbsp />mH and 130<nbsp />mH inductors in parallel.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{(30~\textrm{mH})(130~\textrm{mH})}{30~\textrm{mH}+130~\textrm{mH}} = 24.375~\textrm{mH}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example1-solution2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 0 meshes consisting of 3 inductors. The 10mH connects at node a. The 10mH and 24.375mH connect at node b. The 24.375mH and 40mH connect at node c. The 40mH connects at node d. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=10~mH, o-]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=24.375~mH]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [L, l_=40~mH, o-]		(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All the remaining inductors are in series. Add the values together to calculate the equivalent inductance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L_{EQ} \amp= 10~\textrm{mH} + 40~\textrm{mH} + 24.375~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 74.4~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-equivinduct-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent inductance of the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentinductance-example2-diagram" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentinductance-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-equivinduct-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of seven inductors. The 80uH connects at node a. The 80uH, 50uH, 90uH, and 30uH connect at node b. The 30uH, 50uH, and 40uH connect at node c. The 20uH, 50uH, and 40uH connect at node d. The 20uH, 90uH, and 50uH connect at node e. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [L, l^=80~$\mu$H, o-]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [L, l_=50~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [L, l_=90~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [L, l^=30~$\mu$H]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=50~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [short]				++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(4*\H,\H) to [L, l_=40~$\mu$H]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]				++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [L, l_=20~$\mu$H]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,0) to [short]					++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>L_{EQ} = 102.2~\mu\textrm{H}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by combining the 40<nbsp />&#181;H and 50&#181;H inductors in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{(50~\mu\textrm{H})(40~\mu\textrm{H})}{50~\mu\textrm{H}+40~\mu\textrm{H}} = 22.22~\mu\textrm{H}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example2-solution1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of six inductors. The 80uH connects at node a. The 80uH, 50uH, 90uH, and 30uH connect at node b. The 30uH and 22.2uH connect at node c. The 20uH and 22.2uH connect at node d. The 20uH, 90uH, and 50uH connect at node e. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [L, l^=80~$\mu$H, o-]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [L, l_=50~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [L, l_=90~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [L, l^=30~$\mu$H]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=22.2~$\mu$H]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]				++(right:2*\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,0) to [L, l_=20~$\mu$H]		++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the 30<nbsp />&#181;H, 22.2<nbsp />&#181;H, and 20<nbsp />&#181;H inductors in series.</p>

					<p>
						<md>30~\mu\textrm{H} + 22.2~\mu\textrm{H} + 20~\mu\textrm{H} = 72.2~\mu\textrm{H}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example2-solution2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of four inductors. The 80uH connects at node a. The 80uH, 50uH, 90uH, and 72.2uH connect at node b. The 50uH, 90uH, and 72.2uH connect at node c. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [L, l^=80~$\mu$H, o-]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [L, l_=50~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [short]					++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [L, l_=90~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(2*\H,\H) to [short]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(3*\H,\H) to [L, l_=72.2~$\mu$H]	++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]				++(right:3*\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the 50<nbsp />&#181;H, 90<nbsp />&#181;H, and 72.2<nbsp />&#181;H inductors in parallel.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{1}{\frac{1}{50~\mu\textrm{H}} + \frac{1}{90~\mu\textrm{H}} + \frac{1}{72.2~\mu\textrm{H}}} = 22.24~\mu\textrm{H}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example2-solution3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 0 meshes consisting of two inductors. The 80uH connects at node a. The 80uH and 22.24uH connect at node b. The 22.24uH connects at node c. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\def\H{2.5}
									\draw[]	(0,\H) to [L, l^=80~$\mu$H, o-]		++(right:\H);
									\draw[]	(\H,\H) to [L, l_=22.24~$\mu$H]		++(down:\H);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]				++(right:\H);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the remaining two inductors in series to calculate the total equivalent inductance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L_{EQ} \amp= 80~\mu\textrm{H} + 22.24~\mu\textrm{H}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 102.2~\mu\textrm{H}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-equivinduct-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the value of inductor <m>L_X</m> given that the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentinductance-example3-diagram" /> has an equivalent inductance of 250<nbsp />&#181;H.</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentinductance-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-equivinduct-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of four inductors. The 100uH connects at node a. The 100uH, 200uH, and Lx connect at node b. Lx and 300uH connect at node c. The 200uH and 300uH connect at node d. The equivalent inductance is measured between nodes a and d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l^=100~$\mu$H, o-]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=200~$\mu$H, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l^=$L_X$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=300~$\mu$H]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>L_X = 300~\mu\textrm{H}</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use the equivalent inductance relationships to calculate <m>L_X</m>. The unit used for inductance in the equations below is &#181;H.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L_EQ \amp= 250</mrow>
							<mrow>250 \amp= (300+L_X)//200+100</mrow>
							<mrow>150 \amp= \frac{200(300+L_X)}{200+300+L_X}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{60000 + 200L_X}{500+L_X}</mrow>
							<mrow>75000+150L_X \amp= 60000+200L_X</mrow>
							<mrow>15000 \amp= 50L_X</mrow>
							<mrow>300 \amp= L_X</mrow>
							<mrow>L_X \amp= 300~\mu\textrm{H}</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-equivinduct-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>For the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentinductance-example4-diagram" />, calculate the equivalent inductance of the RL circuit. Then, calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch moves from position a to position b (<m>R_{EQ,ab}</m>), and for when the switch moves from position b to position a (<m>R_{EQ,ba}</m>).</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentinductance-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-equivinduct-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 7 nodes consisting of a 16mA source, 3V source, four resistors, three inductors, and an SPDT switch. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The 16mA source flows out of node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects to the 470ohm resistor at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, 180ohm, and 560ohm resistors connect at node c. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 3.9mH and 5.6mH inductors at node d. The 5.6mH and 5.6mH inductors connect at node e. The 470ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 3V source at node f. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, low-potential end of the 3V source, 180ohm resistor, 3.9mH inductor, and 5.6mH inductor connect at node g. The 16mA source flows into node g. The SPDT switch is depicted as being between the two outputs.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=330~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5.4) |- (10,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]				++(left:5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [I, l_=16~mA]				++(down:5.4) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=470~$\Omega$]	++(down:2.08) to [V, l_=3~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]							++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=3.9~mH, *-*]						++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l^=5.6~mH]								++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [L, l_=5.6~mH]								++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L_{EQ} \amp= 2.89~\textrm{mH}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ab} \amp= 690~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,ba} \amp= 676~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by combining the two series inductors. The equivalent inductance of the two series inductors is</p>

					<p>
						<md>5.6~\textrm{mH} + 5.6~\textrm{mH} = 11.2~\textrm{mH}</md>.
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 16mA source, 3V source, four resistors, an SPDT switch, and two inductors. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, 470ohm resistor, and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The 16mA source flows out of node a. The 470ohm resistor connects to output b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 180ohm and 560ohm resistors at node c. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 3.9mH and 11.2mH inductors at node d. The 470ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 3V source at node e. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, low-potential end of the 3V source, 180ohm resistor, 3.9mH inductor, and 11.2mH inductor connect at node f. The 16mA source flows into node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as being between the two outputs.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=330~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5.4) |- (10,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]				++(left:5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [I, l_=16~mA]				++(down:5.4) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=470~$\Omega$]	++(down:2.08) to [V, l_=3~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]							++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=3.9~mH, *-*]						++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]									++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [L, l_=11.2~mH]								++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two parallel inductors. The equivalent inductance is equal to </p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{(11.2~\textrm{mH})(3.9~\textrm{mH})}{11.2~\textrm{mH} + 3.9~\textrm{mH}} = 2.89~\textrm{mH}</md>.
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 16mA source, a 3V source, four resistors, an SPDT switch, and one inductor. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The 16mA source flows out of node a. The 470ohm resistor connects to output b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 180ohm and 560ohm resistors at node c. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 2.89mH inductor at node d. The 470ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 3V source at node e. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, low-potential end of the 3V source, 180ohm resistor, and 2.89mH inductor connect at node f. The 16mA source flows into node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as being between the two outputs.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt mid, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=330~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5.4) |- (7.5,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]				++(left:5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [I, l_=16~mA]				++(down:5.4) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=470~$\Omega$]	++(down:2.08) to [V, l_=3~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]							++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]							++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch moves to position b can be analyzed with the switch in position b. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 3V source, three resistors, an SPDT switch, and one inductor. Output a of the SPDT switch connects at node a. The 470ohm resistor connects to output b of the SPDT switch at node b. The input of the SPDT switch, 180ohm resistor, and 560ohm resistor connect at node c. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 2.89mH inductor at node d. The 470ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 3V source at node e. The low-potential end of the 3V source, 180ohm resistor, and 2.89mH inductor connect at node f. The SPDT switch is depicted as being in position b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt up, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=470~$\Omega$]	++(down:2.08) to [V, l_=3~V]	++(down:2.3) |- (7.5,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]							++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]					++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]							++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The voltage source should be deactivated by replacing it with a short. The switch will also be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of three resistors and one inductor. The 470ohm, 180ohm, and 560ohm resistors connect at node a. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 2.89mH inductor at node b. The 470ohm and 180ohm resistors connect to the 2.89mH inductor at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=470~$\Omega$]			++(down:2.5) to [short]	++(right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]		++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]				++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of two resistors and one inductor. The 130ohm and 560ohm resistors connect at node a. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 2.89mH inductor at node b. The 2.89mH inductor and 130ohm resistor connect at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=130~$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]		++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the series resistors. This represents the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch moves to position b.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 690ohm resistor and 2.89mH inductor. Both circuit elements are positioned in the same closed circuit sharing a branch and mesh.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=690~$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5) to [short]	++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch moves to position a can be analyzed with the switch in position a. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 16mA source, three resistors, an SPDT switch, and an inductor. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, and output a of the SPDT switch connect at node a. The 16mA source flows out of node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 180ohm and 560ohm resistors at node c. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 2.89mH inductor at node d. The 16mA source, 330ohm resistor, 180ohm resistor, and 2.89mH inductor connect at node e. The 16mA source flows into node e. The SPDT switch is depicted as being in position a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt down, rotate=180, anchor=in] at 					(2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(2.5,0)$) to [R, l_=330~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:5.4) |- (7.5,-2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]					++(left:5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)-(5,0)$) to [I, l_=16~mA]				++(down:5.4) --++ (right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]								++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]						++(down:5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]								++(down:5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The current source should be deactivated by replacing it with an open. The switch will be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of three resistors and one inductor. The 330ohm, 180ohm, and 560ohm resistors connect at node a. The 560ohm resistor connects to the 2.89mH inductor at node b. The 330ohm and 180ohm resistors connect to the 2.89mH inductor at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=330~$\Omega$]				++(down:2.5) --++(right:5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=180~$\Omega$, *-*]		++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]			++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]					++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the parallel resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution9">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of two resistors and one inductor. The 116ohm and 560ohm resistors connect at node a. The 560ohm resistor and 2.89mH inductor connect at node b. The 116ohm resistor and 2.89mH inductor connect at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=116~$\Omega$]	++(down:2.5) --++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=560~$\Omega$]	++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]	++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the series resistors. This represents the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch moves to position a.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example4-solution10">
							<shortdescription>Circuit consisting of 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 676ohm resistor and 2.89mH inductor. Both circuit elements are contained within the same closed mesh.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=676~$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5) --++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					++(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=2.89~mH]		++(down:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-equivinduct-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>For the circuit shown in <xref ref="equivalentinductance-example5-diagram" />, calculate the equivalent inductance of the RL circuit. Then, calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch opens (<m>R_{EQ,open}</m>), and for when the switch closes (<m>R_{EQ,close}</m>).</p>

					<figure xml:id="equivalentinductance-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-equivinduct-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 20V source, 40mA source, three resistors, an SPST switch, and three inductors. The 1.5kohm, 330uH, 680uH, 3.3kohm, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node a. The 680uH and 120uH inductors connect at node b. The 3.3kohm connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 2.2kohm and 40mA source at node d. The 40mA source flows into node d. The 1.5kohm, 330uH, 120uH, low-potential end of the 20V source, 2.2kohm, and 40mA source connect at node e. The 40mA source flows out of node e. The SPST switch is depicted as being open.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]	(7.5,5) to [cute open switch]	++(right:2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=330~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=680~$\mu$H, *-]		++(down:2.5) to [L, l_=120~$\mu$H, -*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$, *-]	++(down:2.5) to [V, l_=20~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]	++(right:12.5) to [I, l^=40~mA]	++(up:5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L_{EQ} \amp= 234~\mu\textrm{H}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,open} \amp= 1.03~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ,close} \amp= 0.702~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by combining the two series inductors. The equivalent inductance of the two series inductors is</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>680~\mu\textrm{H} + 120~\mu\textrm{H} = 800~\mu\textrm{H}</md>.
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a 20V source, 40mA source, three resistors, an SPST switch, and two inductors. The 1.5kohm, 330uH, 800uH, 3.3kohm, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node a. The 3.3kohm connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 2.2kohm and 40mA source at node b. The 40mA source flows into node b. The 1.5kohm, 330uH, 800uH, low-potential end of the 20V source, 2.2kohm, and 40mA source connect at node d. The 40mA source flows out of node d. The SPST switch is depicted as being open.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]		(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]	(7.5,5) to [cute open switch]	++(right:2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=330~$\mu$H, *-*]		++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=800~$\mu$H, *-*]		++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$, *-]	++(down:2.5) to [V, l_=20~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]	++(right:12.5) to [I, l^=40~mA]	++(up:5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two parallel inductors. The equivalent inductance is equal to</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{(330~\mu\textrm{H})(800~\mu\textrm{H})}{330~\mu\textrm{H}+800~\mu\textrm{H}} = 234~\mu\textrm{H}</md>.
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a 20V source, 40mA source, three resistors, an SPST switch, and one inductor. The 1.5kohm, 234uH, 3.3kohm, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node a. The 3.3kohm connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 2.2kohm and 40mA source at node b. The 40mA source flows into node b. The 1.5kohm, 234uH, low-potential end of the 20V source, 2.2kohm, and 40mA source connect at node d. The 40mA source flows out of node d. The SPST switch is depicted as being open.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]	(5,5) to [cute open switch]	++(right:2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H, *-*]						++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$, *-]	++(down:2.5) to [V, l_=20~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]	++(right:10) to [I, l^=40~mA]	++(up:5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch opens can be analyzed with the switch opened. All circuit elements disconnected from the circuit have been removed from the diagram.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 20V source, two resistors, and one inductor. The 1.5kohm, 234uH, and 3.3kohm connect at node a. The 3.3kohm connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node b. The 1.5kohm, 234uH, and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]	(0,0) --++ (right:5);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]					(5,5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$]	++(down:2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]	++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The voltage source should be deactivated by replacing it with a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 1.5kohm resistor, 234uH inductor, and 3.3kohm resistor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]		(0,0) --++ (right:5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the parallel resistors. This represents the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch opens.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 1.03kohm resistor and 234uH inductor wired in the same mesh.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5)	to [R, l_=1.03~k$\Omega$]		(0,0) --++ (right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H]			++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch closes can be analyzed with the switch closed.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 20V source, 40mA source, three resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The 1.5kohm, 234uH, 3.3kohm, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node a. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 2.2kohm and 40mA source at node b. The 40mA source flows into node b. The 3.3kohm connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node c. The 1.5kohm, 234uH, low-potential end of the 20V source, 2.2kohm, and 40mA source connect at node d. The 40mA source flows out of node d. The SPST switch is depicted as being closed.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]	(5,5) to [cute closed switch]	++(right:2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$, *-]	++(down:2.5) to [V, l_=20~V, -*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]	++(right:10) to [I, l^=40~mA]	++(up:5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Both sources should be deactivated. The switch will be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 234uH inductor, and 1.5k, 3.3k, and 2.2k resistors. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5)	to [R, l_=1.5~k$\Omega$]		(0,0) --++ (right:7.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						++(right:7.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=3.3~k$\Omega$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.2~k$\Omega$]		++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the parallel resistors. This represents the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor when the switch closes.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-equivalentinductance-example5-solution8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 0.702kohm resistor and 234uH inductor connected in the same mesh.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5)	to [R, l_=0.702~k$\Omega$]			(0,0) --++ (right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							++ (right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=234~$\mu$H]				++(down:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Inductor Current-Voltage Relationship</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch6-ivinduct-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>The current flowing through a 20<nbsp />mH inductor is <m>i(t) = 50~\textrm{mA/s}~t~u(t)</m>. Derive equations for the voltage dropped over the inductor and the instantaneous power consumed by the inductor.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 0.001~\textrm{V}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= 5\times10^{-5}~\textrm{W}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the voltage drop using <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. Use units of H and A. This means the result will have a unit of V.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.02\frac{d}{dt}\left[0.05t~u(t)\right]</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.02\frac{d}{dt}\left[0.05t\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.02(0.05)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.001~\textrm{V}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the instantaneous power using <xref ref="ohmspower1" />. Use units of A and V. This means the result will have a unit of W.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= i(t)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.05~t~u(t))(0.001~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5\times10^{-5}~\textrm{W}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-ivinduct-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>The voltage dropped over a 15<nbsp />&#181;H inductor is <m>v(t) = 1~\textrm{V}~\cos(5000t)~u(t)</m>. Derive equations for the current through the inductor and the instantaneous power consumed by the inductor.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 13.33~\textrm{A}~\sin(5000t)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= 6.67~\textrm{W}\sin(10000t)~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the current using <xref ref="ch6-currentinductor" />. Use units of H and V. This will cause the result to have a unit of A.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{1}{L}\int_{-\infty}^t v(\tau)\, d\tau</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{15\times10^{-6}}\int_{-\infty}^t \cos(5000\tau)~u(\tau)\, d\tau</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{15\times10^{-6}}\int_{0}^t \cos(5000\tau)\, d\tau~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{(15\times10^{-6})(5000)} \sin(5000\tau) \Big|^t_0~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{0.075} \sin(5000\tau) \Big|^t_0~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.33~\textrm{A}~\left[\sin(5000t)-\sin(0)\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.33~\textrm{A}~\sin(5000t)~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Calculate the instantaneous power using <xref ref="ohmspower1" />. Use units of A and V so that the result has a unit of W.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= i(t)v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (13.33~\sin(5000t)~u(t))(1~\cos(5000t)~u(t))</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the double angle formula: <m>\sin(x)~\cos(x) = \frac{1}{2}~\sin(2x)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>p(t) = 6.67~\textrm{W}\sin(10000t)~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-ivinduct-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>The voltage dropped over a 10<nbsp />mH inductor is <m>v(t) = 10~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-2500t}~u(t)</m>. Derive equations for the current through the inductor and the instantaneous power consumed by the inductor.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.4~\textrm{A}\left(1-\textrm{e}^{-2500t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= 4~\textrm{W}\left(\textrm{e}^{-2500t}-\textrm{e}^{-5000t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Calculate the current using <xref ref="ch6-currentinductor" />. Use units of H and V. This will cause the result to have a unit of A.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \frac{1}{L}\int_{-\infty}^t v(\tau)\, d\tau</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{0.01}\int_{-\infty}^t 10~\textrm{e}^{-2500\tau}~u(\tau)\, d\tau</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{0.01}\int_{0}^t 10~\textrm{e}^{-2500\tau}\, d\tau~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{(0.01)(-2500)}\left(10~\textrm{e}^{-2500\tau}\right)\Big|^t_0~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{(0.01)(-2500)}\left(10~\textrm{e}^{-2500t}-10\right)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.4~\textrm{A}~\left(1-\textrm{e}^{-2500t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower1" /> to calculate <m>p(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>p(t) \amp= \left(10~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-2500t}~u(t)\right)\left(0.4~\textrm{A}~\left(1-\textrm{e}^{-2500t}\right)~u(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{W}\left(\textrm{e}^{-2500t}-\textrm{e}^{-5000t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-ivinduct-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>A 50<nbsp />mH inductor has current as shown in <xref ref="inductor-ivrelationship-example3-diagram" />. Derive the piecewise functions for <m>i(t)</m>, <m>v(t)</m>, and <m>p(t)</m>. Assume that current is zero for all time before 0<nbsp />ms.</p>

					<figure xml:id="inductor-ivrelationship-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Graph corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-ivinduct-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-inductor-ivrelationship-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted with respect to time in ms. The graph is linear from (0,0) to (5,10) then linear to (7.5,0) and then remains zero.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[xlabel=$t$~(ms), ylabel=$i(t)$~(mA), xmin=0, xmax=10, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, ymin=-5, ymax=15, ytick={-5,0,...,15}]
										\addplot[thick, domain=0:5, samples=5] {2*x};
										\addplot[thick, domain=5:7.5, samples=5] {-4*x+30};
										\addplot[thick, domain=7.5:10]	{0};
										\addplot[dashed, domain=0:5] {10};
										\addplot[dashed, domain=0:7.5] {0};
										\addplot[dashed] coordinates {(5,10) (5,-5)};
	    							\end{axis}
								\end{tikzpicture}	
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>
								i(t) \amp=
									\begin{cases}
										2000~\textrm{mA/s}~t \amp \text{if } 0~\textrm{ms} \leq t \lt 5~\textrm{ms}\\
										-4000~\textrm{mA/s}~t + 30~\textrm{mA} \amp \text{if } 5~\textrm{ms} \leq t \lt 7.5~\textrm{ms}\\
										0 \amp \text{else}
									\end{cases}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								v(t) \amp=
									\begin{cases}
										0.1~\textrm{V} \amp \text{if } 0~\textrm{ms} \leq t \lt 5~\textrm{ms}\\
										-0.2~\textrm{V} \amp \text{if } 5~\textrm{ms} \leq t \lt 7.5~\textrm{ms}\\
										0 \amp \text{else}
									\end{cases}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								p(t) \amp=
									\begin{cases}
										200~\textrm{mW/s}~t \amp \text{if } 0~\textrm{ms} \leq t \lt 5~\textrm{ms}\\
										800~\textrm{mW/s}~t - 6~\textrm{mW} \amp \text{if } 5~\textrm{ms} \leq t \lt 7.5~\textrm{ms}\\
										0 \amp \text{else}
									\end{cases}
							</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Between 0<nbsp />ms and 5<nbsp />ms, the current increases from 0<nbsp />mA to 10<nbsp />mA. This represents a slope of </p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{10~\textrm{mA}-0~\textrm{mA}}{0.005~\textrm{s}-0~\textrm{s}} = 2000~\textrm{mA/s}</md>.
					</p>
					
					<p>The function <m>i(t)</m> is therefore equal to <m>2000~\textrm{mA/s}~t</m> during this interval of time.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to calculate the voltage during this time interval.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0.05~\textrm{H}\frac{d}{dt}(2~\textrm{A/s}~t) = 0.05~\textrm{H}(2~\textrm{A/s}) = 0.1~\textrm{V}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower1" /> to calculate the power consumed by the inductor during this time period.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>(0.1~\textrm{V})(2000~\textrm{mA/s}~t) = 200~\textrm{mW/s}~t</md>
					</p>

					<p>Between 5<nbsp />ms and 7.5<nbsp />ms, the current decreases from 10<nbsp />mA to 0<nbsp />mA. This represents a slope of </p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{0~\textrm{mA}-10~\textrm{mA}}{0.075~\textrm{s}-0.005~\textrm{s}} = -4000~\textrm{mA/s}</md>.
					</p>
					
					<p>If this function originated at zero, the y-intercept would be 10<nbsp />mA, representing a function of <m>-4000~\textrm{mA/s}~t + 10~\textrm{mA}</m>. However, the function is shifted by 5<nbsp />ms (or 0.005<nbsp />s). The function <m>i(t)</m> is therefore equal to <m>-4000~\textrm{mA/s}~(t-0.005)+10~\textrm{mA} = -4000~\textrm{mA/s}~t + 30~\textrm{mA}</m> during this interval of time.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> to calculate the voltage during this time interval.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>0.05~\textrm{H}\frac{d}{dt}(-4~\textrm{A/s}~t + 0.03~\textrm{A}) = 0.05~\textrm{H}(-4~\textrm{A/s}) = -0.2~\textrm{V}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower1" /> to calculate the power consumed by the inductor during this time period.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>(-0.2~\textrm{V})(-4000~\textrm{mA/s}~t + 30~\textrm{mA}) = 800~\textrm{mW/s}~t - 6~\textrm{mW}</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-ivinduct-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p><xref ref="ivrelationship-inductors-example5-diagram" /> depicts <m>i(t)</m> for a 56<nbsp />&#181;H inductor. Calculate <m>\tau</m>, <m>R_{EQ}</m> as seen by the inductor, and an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="ivrelationship-inductors-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Graph corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-ivinduct-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-ivrelationship-inductors-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Graph of i(t) in mA plotted as a function of t in ms. The graph starts at (0,600) and exponentially decays to 400. A data point is highlighted at (2,500).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\begin{axis}[ylabel={$i(t)$~(mA)}, xlabel={$t$~($\mu$s)}, width=\linewidth, height=5cm, xmin=-0.5, xmax=20, ymin=0, ymax=700, ytick={0,100,...,700}]
										\addplot[black, thick] coordinates {(-0.5,600) (0,600)};
										\addplot[black, thick, domain=0:20, samples=50] {400+200*exp(-x*0.3466)};
										\addplot[only marks, mark=square] coordinates {(2,500)};
										\coordinate (a1) at (axis cs:-0.5,500);
										\coordinate (a2) at (axis cs:2,500);
										\coordinate (a3) at (axis cs:2,0);
										\coordinate (b1) at (axis cs:-0.5,400);
										\coordinate (b2) at (axis cs:20,400);
	    							\end{axis}
								\draw[thick, dashed]	(a1) -- (a2) -- (a3);
								\draw[thick, dashed]	(b1) -- (b2);
							\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= 2.89~\mu\textrm{s}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 19.4~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -3.88~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-347000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the known coordinate (2<nbsp />&#181;s, 500<nbsp />mA) using <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>. (Because a unit of &#181;s is used for the variable <m>t</m>, the unit of <m>\tau</m> in the calculation will also be &#181;s.) Only the time period where <m>t \geq 0</m> will be considered.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left(400~\textrm{mA} + 200~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-t/\tau}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>i(2) = 500~\textrm{mA} \amp= \left(400~\textrm{mA} + 200~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-2/\tau}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>100~\textrm{mA} \amp= 200~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-2/\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>0.5 \amp= \textrm{e}^{-2/\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>\ln{0.5} \amp= \ln{\textrm{e}^{-2/\tau}}</mrow>
							<mrow>-0.6931 \amp= \frac{-2}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{2}{0.6931}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.89~\mu\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R \amp= \frac{L}{\tau}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{56 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{H}}{2.89\times 10^{-6}~\textrm{s}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 19.4~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (0.0194~\textrm{k}\Omega)\left(-200~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-347000t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -3.88~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-347000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>RL Circuits: Homogeneous</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneousrlcircuit-example1-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneousrlcircuit-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlcircuit-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 6V source, three resistors, one inductor, and an SPDT switch. The 300ohm resistor connects to output a of the SPDT switch at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects to the 700ohm resistor at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 900ohm resistor at node c. The 900ohm resistor connects to the 320mH inductor at node d. The 300ohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 6V source at node e. The low-potential end of the 6V source connects to the 700ohm resistor and 320mH inductor at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node f.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[above] {a} node[cute spdt up, xscale=-1, anchor=out 1]	(switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) to [R, l_=300~$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=6~V] ++(down:3) |- ($(switch.out 2)-(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[left] {b} to [R, l_=700~$\Omega$, -*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [R, l^=900~$\Omega$]		++(right:2.5) to [L, l_=320~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	++(down:2.75) |- ($(switch.out 2)-(0,2.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-5000t}~u(t) + 5~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-5000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short. The 700<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is not connected to the circuit in this condition.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlcircuit-example1-solution1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 6V source, 300ohm resistor, and 900ohm resistor. All three circuit elements are wired in series. Branch current i(0) is defined as flowing through the resistors from the high-potential end of the source to the low-potential end of the source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=6~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=300~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=900~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(0)$, i_=$i(0)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{6~\textrm{V}}{300~\Omega+900~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.005~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlcircuit-example1-solution2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 700ohm resistor, 900ohm resistor, and 320mH inductor. All three circuit elements are wired in series. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor with the same polarity as the voltage.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=700~$\Omega$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=900~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=320~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 700~\Omega+900~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1600~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{L}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.32~\textrm{H}}{1600~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2\times10^{-4}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-5000t}~u(t) + 5~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -(1600~\Omega)(0.005~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-5000t}~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -8~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-5000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneousrlcircuit-example2-diagram" />. The switch is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneousrlcircuit-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrlcircuit-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 5V source, six resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm, 750ohm, and 500ohm resistors connect at node b. The 500ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node c. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 20mH inductor and 30ohm resistor at node d. The 30ohm, 600ohm, and 150ohm resistors connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 5V source, 750ohm, 600ohm, and 150ohm resistors, and 20mH inductor connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=750~$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]						(6.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [L, l_=20~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]						(9,2.5);
									\draw[]	(9,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, *-*]					(9,0);
									\draw[]	(9,2.5) to [short]										(11.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(11.5,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$]						(11.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(11.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 6~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-7500t}~u(t) + 6~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -0.9~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-7500t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-example2-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 5V source, six resistors, and a short. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm, 750ohm, and 500ohm resistors connect at node b. The 500ohm resistor, 30ohm resistor, and short connect at node c. The 30ohm, 600ohm, and 150ohm resistors connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 5V source, 750ohm, 600ohm, and 150ohm resistors, and the short connect at node e. Voltage v(0) drops over the short from node c to node e. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node c to node e.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=750~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(0)$, i_=$i(0)$, *-*]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, *-*]			(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$]				(10,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 30<nbsp />&#8486;, 600<nbsp />&#8486;, and 150<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are shorted by the inductor and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-example2-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 5V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm, 750ohm, and 500ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 5V source connects to the 750ohm and 500ohm resistors at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the 500ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=750~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(0)$, i_=$i(0)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use source transformation to convert the voltage source to a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{5~\textrm{V}}{200~\Omega} = 0.025~\textrm{A}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-example2-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with a 0.025A source, 200ohm resistor, 750ohm resistor, and 500ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The current source flows out of node b and into node a. Branch current i(0) flows through the 500ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=0.025~A]					(-2.5,2.5) to [short] ++(right:5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]		(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=750~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, v^=$v(0)$, i_=$i(0)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{200~\Omega} + \frac{1}{750~\Omega} + \frac{1}{500~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 120~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 0.025~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{120~\Omega}{500~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.006~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-example2-solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an inductor and three resistors. The 20mH inductor and 30ohm resistor connect at node a. The 30ohm, 600ohm, and 150ohm resistors connect at node b. The 20mH inductor connect to the 600ohm and 150ohm resistors at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=20~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]		(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 600~\Omega//150~\Omega+30~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 120~\Omega+30~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 150~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{L}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.02~\textrm{H}}{150~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.33\times10^{-4}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 6~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-7500t}~u(t) + 6~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -(150~\Omega)\left(0.006~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-7500t}~u(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.9~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-7500t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-rl-example3-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-rl-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-rl-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 120V source, four resistors, inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 120V source connects to the 30ohm resistor at node a. The 30ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The 20ohm resistor connects to the 2uH inductor at node c. The 2uH inductor connects to the 40ohm resistor at node d. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 16ohm and 40ohm resistors at node e. The 16ohm resistor connects to the low-potential end of the 120V source at node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=120~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=2~$\mu$H, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]		(2.5,0) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 1.13~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-30000000t}~u(t) + 1.13~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -67.9~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-30000000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example3-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 120V source and four resistors (30ohm, 20ohm, 40ohm, and 16ohm). All circuit elements are wired in series. Branch current i(0) flows through the circuit from the high-potential end of the source to the low-potential end of the source.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=120~V]			(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]		(2.5,0) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{120~\textrm{V}}{30~\Omega + 20~\Omega + 16~\Omega + 40~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{120~\textrm{V}}{106~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.13~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example3-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 120V source, four resistors, one inductor, and a short. The high-potential end of the 120V source connects to the 30ohm resistor at node a. The 30ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and short connect at node b. The 20ohm resistor connects to the 2uH inductor at node c. The 2uH inductor connects to the 40ohm resistor at node d. The 40ohm resistor, 16ohm resistor, and short connect at node e. The low-potential end of the 120V source connects to the 16ohm resistor at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=120~V]			(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, *-*]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=2~$\mu$H, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=16~$\Omega$]		(2.5,0) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The voltage source, 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, and 16<nbsp />&#8486; resistor are shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="30%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example3-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 20ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and 2uH inductor. All circuit elements are wired in series.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~$\Omega$] ++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=2~$\mu$H, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]	++(right:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is equal to the series combination of both resistors, which is 60<nbsp />&#8486;. Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{2 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{H}}{60~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.33 \times 10^{-8}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 1.13~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-30000000t}~u(t) + 1.13~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -(60~\Omega)(1.13~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-30000000t}~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -67.9~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-30000000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-rl-example4-diagram" />. The switch has been closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-rl-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-rl-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 1.8V source, three resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 1.8V source connects to the 30ohm resistor at node a. The 30ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch, 20ohm and 40ohm resistors connect at node c. The 40ohm resistor connects to the 800mH inductor at node d. The 800mH inductor, 20ohm resistor, and low-potential end of the 1.8V source connect at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the 20ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node c to node e. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=1.8~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=800~mH, i^=$i_L(t)$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -13.8~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-75}~u(t) + 27.7~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -0.831~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-75}~u(t) + 1.66~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short. Because the current through the resistor may be discontinuous with time, both <m>i(0^-)</m> and <m>i_L(0)</m> should be calculated.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example4-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 1.8V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 1.8V source connects to the 30ohm resistor at node a. The 30ohm, 20ohm, and 40ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 1.8V source connects to the 20ohm and 40ohm resistors at node c. Voltage v(0-) drops over the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(0-) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 40ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=1.8~V]										(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=30~$\Omega$]									(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^-)$, i_=$i(0^-)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i_L(0)$]									(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{1.8~\textrm{V}}{30~\Omega} = 0.06~\textrm{A} = 60~\textrm{mA}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example4-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 60mA source, 30ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and 40ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 60mA source flows out of node b and into node a. Voltage v(0-) drops over the 20ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current i(0-) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node a to node b. Branch current iL(0) flows through the 40ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=60~mA]										(-2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=30~$\Omega$, *-*]								(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^-)$, i_=$i(0^-)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]								(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i^=$i_L(0)$]									(5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]												(5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{30~\Omega} + \frac{1}{20~\Omega} + \frac{1}{40~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9.23~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0^-)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0^-) \amp= 60~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{9.23~\Omega}{20~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 27.7~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i_L(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i_L(0) \amp= 60~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{9.23~\Omega}{40~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 13.8~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate <m>i(0^+)</m>. The inductor can be treated as a current source with a value of <m>i_L(0)</m>. The voltage source and 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistor are disconnected from the circuit at this time.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example4-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 13.8mA source and two resistors. The 20ohm and 40ohm resistors connect at node a. The 40ohm resistor and 13.8mA source connect at node b. The 13.8mA source flows out of node b. The 13.8mA source connects to the 20ohm resistor at node c. The 13.8mA source flows into node c. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 20ohm resistor from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, i_=$i(0^+)$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [I, l_=13.8~mA]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Due to KCL, <m>i(0^+)</m> must be equal to <minus />13.8<nbsp />mA.</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The voltage source and 30<nbsp />&#8486; resistor are disconnected from the circuit at this time.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example4-solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 20ohm resistor, 40ohm resistor, and 800mH inductor, all wired in series.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=40~$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=800~mH]								(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance is equal to the series combination of both resistors, which is 60<nbsp />&#8486;. Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.8~\textrm{H}}{60~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.013~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = -13.8~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-75}~u(t) + 27.7~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use Ohm's law to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (0.06~\textrm{k}\Omega)(-13.8~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-75}~u(t) + 27.7~\textrm{mA}~u(-t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -0.831~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-75}~u(t) + 1.66~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> for the circuit shown in <xref ref="homogeneous-rl-example5-diagram" />. The switch has been closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds. The parameter values are <m>r_m = 8~\textrm{V/A}</m>, <m>L = 6~\textrm{H}</m>, <m>R_1 = 1~\Omega</m>, and <m>R_2 = 4~\Omega</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="homogeneous-rl-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-rl-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 0.4V source, five resistors, inductor, SPST switch, and CCVS rm*iX(t). The high-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 1ohm resistor at node a. The 1ohm, 2ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node c. The other end of the SPST switch, inductor L, and high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node d. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node e. The low-potential end of the 0.4V source, 2ohm resistor, L, R1, and R2 connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node d to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node f. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as opening.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=0.4~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cute opening switch]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$]					(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i_=$i_X(t)$, *-*]			(10,0);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [short]									(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]							(12.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(12.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 40~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-1.2t}~u(t) + 40~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -288~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-1.2t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 0.4V source, five resistors, rm*iX(0) CCVS, and a short. The high-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 1ohm resistor at node a. The 1ohm, 2ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The 6ohm resistor, high-potential end of the CCVS, and short connect at node c. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node d. The low-potential end of the 0.4V source, 2ohm resistor, short, and R1 and R2 connect at node e. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node c to node e. Branch current iX(0) flows through R1 from node d to node e.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=0.4~V]						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$, *-*]				(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(0)$]					(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i_=$i_X(0)$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]							(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						(10,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The CCVS and resistors <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m> are shorted out of the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 0.4V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 1ohm resistor at node a. The 1ohm, 2ohm, and 6ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 0.4V source connects to the 2ohm and 6ohm resistors at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the 6ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=0.4~V]				(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{0.4~\textrm{V}}{1~\Omega} = 0.4~\textrm{A}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 0.4A source, 1ohm resistor, 2ohm resistor, and 6ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The current source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(0) flows through the 6ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=0.4~A]	(-2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=1~$\Omega$, *-*]		(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~$\Omega$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=6~$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]	(2.5,2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{1~\Omega} + \frac{1}{2~\Omega} + \frac{1}{6~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.6~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 0.4~\textrm{A}\frac{0.6~\Omega}{6~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.04~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 40~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m>. The voltage source, 1<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, 2<nbsp />&#8486; resistor, and 6<nbsp />&#8486; resistor are disconnected from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an inductor L, CCVS rm*iX(t), and two resistors R1 and R2. L and the high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node b. L, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i_=$i_X(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>There are two methods that can be used to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The first method that will be used is to derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a CCVS (rm*iX(t)), and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of the CCVS connects at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node c. R1 and R2 connect at node b. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node c to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$, o-]				(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i_=$i_X(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the test source method to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a voltage source Vtest, CCVS (rm*iX(t)), and resistors R1 and R2. The high-potential end of Vtest and the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node c. The low-potential end of Vtest, R1, and R2 connect at node b. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node c to node b. Branch current Itest flows through Vtest from node b to node a.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{TEST}$, i_=$I_{TEST}$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i_=$i_X(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the left-hand mesh to derive an equation for <m>V_{TEST}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{TEST} \amp= r_mi_X(t) + R_1i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (r_m + R_1)i_X(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting all three circuit branches to derive an equation for <m>I_{TEST}</m>. (Note that the voltage dropped over <m>R_2</m> is equal to the voltage dropped over <m>R_1</m>, which is equal to <m>R_1i_X(t)</m>.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{TEST} \amp= i_X(t) + \frac{R_1}{R_2} i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(1 + \frac{R_1}{R_2}\right)i_X(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{TEST}}{I_{TEST}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(r_m + R_1)i_X(t)}{\left(1 + \frac{R_1}{R_2}\right)i_X(t)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{r_m + R_1}{1 + \frac{R_1}{R_2}}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Plug in numerical quantities to derive this value.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V/A} + 1~\Omega}{1 + \frac{1~\Omega}{4~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{9~\Omega}{1 + 0.25}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.2~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{6~\textrm{H}}{7.2~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.833~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The second method that can be used to solve the circuit is to derive a differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-homogeneousrl-example5-solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an inductor L, CCVS rm*iX(t), and two resistors R1 and R2. L and the high-potential end of the CCVS connect at node a. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to R1 and R2 at node b. L, R1, and R2 connect at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node a to node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cV, l^=$r_mi_X(t)$]					(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, i_=$i_X(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the left-hand mesh.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex5-kvlequation">L\frac{di(t)}{dt} = r_mi_X(t) + R_1i_X(t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting all three circuit branches. (Note that the voltage dropped over <m>R_2</m> is equal to the voltage dropped over <m>R_1</m>, which is equal to <m>R_1i_X(t)</m>.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-homogeneous-ex5-kclequation">i(t) + i_X(t) + \frac{R_1}{R_2}i_X(t) = 0</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Solve <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex5-kclequation" /> for <m>i_X(t)</m> and simplify.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= -i_X(t) - \frac{R_1}{R_2}i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -i_X(t) \left(1 + \frac{R_1}{R_2}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_X(t) \amp= -\left(\frac{1}{1+\frac{R_1}{R_2}}\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\left(\frac{1}{\frac{R_1+R_2}{R_2}}\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\right)i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Plug the simplified equation for <m>i_X(t)</m> into <xref ref="ch6-homogeneous-ex5-kvlequation" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>L\frac{di(t)}{dt} \amp= -(r_m + R_1)\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + (r_m + R_1)\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\right)i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{(r_m + R_1)\left(\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\right)}{L}i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Plug in numerical quantities to obtain the differential equation.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{(8 + 1)\left(\frac{4}{1+4}\right)}{6}i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{(9)(0.8)}{6}i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{7.2}{6}i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + 1.2i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>This yields the same result as the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit method, as expected. If we had not already derived the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, we could use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to solve for <m>R</m>.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 40~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-1.2t}~u(t) + 40~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-homogeneousrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -(7.2~\Omega)(40~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-1.2}~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= -288~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-1.2t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>RL Circuits: Charging from Zero</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch6-charging-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-charging-example1-diagram" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rl-charging-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-charging-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-rl-charging-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an 8V source, four resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 4k resistor at node a. The 4k resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 5k, 6k, and 20k resistors at node c. The 6k resistor connects to the 100mH inductor at node d. The low-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 5k and 20k resistors and 100mH inductor at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=8~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute closing switch]						(4,5);
									\draw[]	(4,5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(4,0);
									\draw[]	(4,5) to [short]										(6.5,5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-]						(6.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [L, l_=100~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(6.5,5) to [short]										(9,5);
									\draw[]	(9,5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$]							(9,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(9,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.5~\textrm{mA}~\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-80000t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-80000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>
					
					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example1-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 8V source and four resistors. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 4k resistor at node a. The 4k, 5k, 6k, and 20k resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 5k, 6k, and 20k resistors at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 6k resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]								(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use source transformation to convert the voltage source to a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{8~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 2~\textrm{mA}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example1-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 2mA source, 4k resistor, 5k resistor, 6k resistor, and 20k resistor. All five circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 2mA source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 6k resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=2~mA]							(-2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{1}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{1}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{1}{20~\textrm{k}\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= 2~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{1.5~\textrm{k}\Omega}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>=\amp= 0.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Draw the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> and deactivate the source to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example1-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of four resistors and an inductor. The 4k, 5k, 6k, and 20k resistors connect at node a. The 6k resistor connects to the 100mH inductor at node b. The 4k, 5k, 20k resistors and 100mH inductor are connected at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]									(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]						(2.5,5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]									(7.5,5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-]					(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=100~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$]						(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 4~\textrm{k}\Omega//5~\textrm{k}\Omega//20~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{L}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.1~\textrm{H}}{8000~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.25\times10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 0.5~\textrm{mA}~\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-80000t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltage" /> to calculate <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (8~\textrm{k}\Omega)(0.5~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-80000t}~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-80000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-charging-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-charging-example2-diagram" />. The switch has been in position b for a very long time before moving to position a at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rl-charging-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-charging-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-rl-charging-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 2.7V source, four resistors, one inductor, and SPDT switch. The 78ohm resistor connects to output a of the SPDT switch at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects to the 52ohm resistor at node b. The input of the SPDT switch connects to the 760mH inductor at node c. The 52ohm, 24ohm, and 7ohm resistors connect at node d. The low-potential end of the 2.7V source connects to the 760mH inductor and 24ohm and 7ohm resistors at node e. The high-potential end of the 2.7V source connects to the 78ohm resistor at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node e. The SPDT switch is depicted as switching from position b to position a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[cute spdt down arrow, rotate=90, anchor=in]	at (2.5,2.5) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[above] {a} to [R, l_=78~$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=2.7~V] ++(down:3) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2.5)$);
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {b} to [R, l^=52~$\Omega$]		++(right:2.5) to [R, l_=24~$\Omega$, *-*] ++(down:3.2);
									\draw[]	(switch.in) to [L, l_=760~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	++(down:2.5);
									\draw[]	($(switch.out 2)+(2.5,0)$) to [short]	++(right:2.5) to [R, l_=7~$\Omega$] ++(down:3) |- ($(switch.in)-(0,2.5)$);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 0.0346~\textrm{A}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-102.6t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 2.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-102.6t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example2-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 2.7V source in series with a 78ohm resistor. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the circuit from the high-potential end of the source to the low-potential end of the source.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=78~$\Omega$]		++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=2.7~V] ++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$]	++(down:2.5) to [short] ++(left:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{2.7~\textrm{V}}{78~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0346~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example2-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 2.7V source, 78ohm resistor, and 760mH inductor, all connected in series.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=78~$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [V, l_=2.7~V] ++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=760~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	++(down:2.5) to [short] ++ (left:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Deactivate the voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example2-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 78ohm resistor and 760mH inductor wired in series.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=78~$\Omega$]	++(left:2.5) to [short] ++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=760~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	++(down:2.5) to [short] ++ (left:2.5);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor is 78<nbsp />&#8486;. Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.760~\textrm{H}}{78~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0097~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 0.0346~\textrm{A}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-102.6t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (78~\Omega)\left(0.0346~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-102.6t}~u(t)\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-102.6t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-charging-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-charging-example3-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rl-charging-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-charging-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-rl-charging-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 10V source, four resistors, inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 250mH inductor and 500ohm (A) resistor at node c. The 500ohm (A) and 500ohm (B) resistors connect at node d. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node e. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm resistor and 500ohm (B) resistor at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=10~V] 				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute closing switch]	(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=250~mH, *-]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=250~$\Omega$, -*, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$] (5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$]		(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=500~$\Omega$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 20~\textrm{mA}~\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-1667t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 5~\textrm{V}~\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-1667t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, simplify the circuit by combining the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example3-solution1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of a 10V source, three resistors, inductor, and SPST switch. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor connects to one end of the SPST switch at node b. The other end of the SPST switch connects to the 250mH inductor and 1000ohm resistor at node c. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node d. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm resistor and 1000ohm resistor at node e. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node d to node e. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node d to node e. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=10~V] 										(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]								(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cute closing switch]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [L, l_=250~mH, *-]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=250~$\Omega$, -*, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$] 	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]											(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=1000~$\Omega$]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example3-solution2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 10V source and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm, 250ohm, and 1000ohm resistors connect at node b. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm and 1000ohm resistors at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V] 									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=250~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$] 		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1000~$\Omega$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use source transformation to convert the voltage source into a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{10~\textrm{V}}{0.2~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 50~\textrm{mA}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example3-solution3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 50mA source, 200ohm resistor, 250ohm resistor, and 1000ohm resistor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 50mA source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=50~mA] 									(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=250~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$] 		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1000~$\Omega$]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{200~\Omega}+\frac{1}{250~\Omega}+\frac{1}{1000~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 100~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The voltage <m>v(\infty)</m> is dropped in parallel with all three resistors. Use Ohm's law with the source current and <m>R_{EQ}</m> to calculate this value.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= (0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(50~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law through the branch with the 250<nbsp />&#8486; resistor to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{5~\textrm{V}}{0.25~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example3-solution4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 10V source, three resistors, and one inductor. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node a. The 200ohm resistor, 250mH inductor, and 1000ohm resistor connect at node b. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node c. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 250ohm and 1000ohm resistors at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [V, l_=10~V] 											(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]									(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=250~mH, *-]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=250~$\Omega$, -*, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$] 	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]												(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=1000~$\Omega$]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Deactivate the voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example3-solution5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of three resistors and one inductor. The 200ohm resistor, 250mH inductor, and 1000ohm resistor connect at node a. The 250mH inductor connects to the 250ohm resistor at node b. The 200ohm, 250ohm, and 1000ohm resistors at node c. Voltage v(t) drops over the 250ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through the 250ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short] 												(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]									(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=250~mH, *-]									(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=250~$\Omega$, -*, i_=$i(t)$, v^=$v(t)$] 	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]												(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=1000~$\Omega$]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (200~\Omega // 1000~\Omega) + 250~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 166.67~\Omega + 250~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 416.67~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.25~\textrm{H}}{416.67~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6 \times 10^{-4}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 20~\textrm{mA}~\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-1667t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (0.25~\textrm{k}\Omega)(20~\textrm{mA}\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-1667t}\right]~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}~\left[1 - \textrm{e}^{-1667t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-charging-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="chargingrlcircuit-example4-diagram" />. The switch is in position a for a very long time before moving to position b at a time of zero seconds. Assume the op-amp is sufficiently sourced such that the output will not saturate. The parameter values are <m>L = 100~\textrm{mH}</m>, <m>R_F = 330~\Omega</m>, and <m>V_S = 0.8~\textrm{V}</m>. The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 70<nbsp />&#8486;.</p>

					<figure xml:id="chargingrlcircuit-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-charging-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrlcircuit-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs, SPDT switch, inductor L, and resistor RF. Output a of the SPDT switch connects to ground at node a. Output b of the SPDT switch connects to the high-potential end of Vs at node b. The-low potential end of Vs connects to ground (node a). The input of the SPDT switch connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node c. L, RF, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node d. The other end of L connects to ground (node a). The other end of RF connects to the output of the op-amp at node e. Voltage v(t) is measured between node e and ground.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
									\node[cute spdt down arrow, rotate=180, anchor=in]	at (opamp.+) (switch) {};
									\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[ground] {} to [L, l^=$L$, -*]			++(right:2.5);
									\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_F$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
									\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]		++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 1) node[left] {b} to [V, l_=$V_S$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\draw[]	(switch.out 2) node[above] {a} to [short]	++(left:0.5) node[ground] {};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 4.57~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-700t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="50%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example4-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs and resistors RP and RF. The high-potential end of Vs connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node a. The low-potential end of Vs connects to ground (node d). RP, RF, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. The other end of RP connects to ground (node d). The other end of RF connects to the output of the op-amp at node c. Voltage v(t) is measured between node c and ground.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(2.5,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=$R_P$, -*]			++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_F$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]		++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(\infty)$};
										\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=$V_S$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>This is a non-inverting op-amp circuit. Use <xref ref="ch4-noninvertingopampgaineq" /> to calculate the gain of the op-amp.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>G \amp= 1 + \frac{R_F}{R_P}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1 + \frac{330~\Omega}{70~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.714</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch4-amplifiergainequation" /> to calculate <m>v(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(\infty) \amp= GV_S</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (5.714)(0.8~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.57~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m>. The parasitic resistance will be explicitly drawn in this circuit to make it clear.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="70%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example4-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of an op-amp, voltage source Vs, inductor L, and resistors RP and RF. The high-potential end of Vs connects to the non-inverting input of the op-amp at node a. The low-potential end of Vs connects to ground (node e). L, Rf, and the inverting input of the op-amp connect at node b. The other end of RF connects to the output of the op-amp at node c. L and RP connect at node d. The other end of RP connects to ground (node e). Voltage v(t) is measured between node c and ground. Branch current iL(t) flows through L from node d to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) node[op amp] (opamp) {};
										\draw[]	($(opamp.-)-(5,0)$) node[ground] {} to [R, l^=$R_P$] ++(right:2.5) to [L, l^=$L$, i^=$i_L(t)$, -*]			++(right:2.5);
										\draw[]	(opamp.-) to [short] ++(up:1.25) to [R, l^=$R_F$]	++(right:2.25) -| (opamp.out);
										\draw[]	(opamp.out) to [short, *-o]		++(right:0.5) node[right] {$v(t)$};
										\draw[]	(opamp.+) to [V, l_=$V_S$]	++(down:2.5) node[tlground] {};
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Derive a differential equation in terms of <m>v(t)</m>. Use KCL at the inverting input of the op-amp.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example4-kcl">i_L(t) = \frac{V_S - v(t)}{R_F}</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KVL over the inductor and its parasitic resistance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example4-kvl">0 - V_S = R_Pi_L(t) + L\frac{di_L(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-chargingrl-example4-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch6-chargingrl-example4-kvl" />, and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>-V_S \amp= R_P\left(\frac{V_S - v(t)}{R_F}\right) + L\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{V_S - v(t)}{R_F}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_P}{R_F}V_S - \frac{R_P}{R_F}v(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}(V_S) - L\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{v(t)}{R_F}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{R_P}{R_F}V_S - \frac{R_P}{R_F}v(t) - L\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{v(t)}{R_F}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>-V_S - \frac{R_P}{R_F}V_S \amp= - \frac{R_P}{R_F}v(t) - L\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{v(t)}{R_F}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>V_S + \frac{R_P}{R_F}V_S \amp= \frac{R_P}{R_F}v(t) + L\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{v(t)}{R_F}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>V_S + \frac{R_P}{R_F}V_S \amp= \frac{R_P}{R_F}v(t) + \frac{L}{R_F}\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{R_F}{L}\right)V_S + \left(\frac{R_F}{L}\right)\frac{R_P}{R_F}V_S \amp= \left(\frac{R_F}{L}\right)\frac{R_P}{R_F}v(t) + \left(\frac{R_F}{L}\right)\frac{L}{R_F}\frac{dv(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{R_F}{L} V_S + \frac{R_P}{L}V_S \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_P}{L}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{R_F + R_P}{L}\right)V_S \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_P}{L}v(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>3200 \amp= \frac{dv(t)}{dt} + 700 v(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 4.57~\textrm{V}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-700t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-charging-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-charging-example5-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The circuit parameters are <m>V_S = 9.7~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_1 = 8~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 60~\textrm{k}\Omega</m>, <m>L = 8.2~\textrm{H}</m>, and <m>k_i = 0.22</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="rl-charging-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-charging-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-rl-charging-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of a voltage source Vs, SPST switch, two resistors, an inductor, and a CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to the SPST switch at node a. The other end of the SPST switch connects to R1 at node b. R1, R2, the CCCS, and L connect at node c. The CCCS flows into node c. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, L, and the CCCS connect at node d. The CCCS flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node c to node d. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node b to node c. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [cute closing switch]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*] 					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= 1.48~\textrm{mA}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-0.7209t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 8.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.7209t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a voltage source Vs, two resistors, a short, and a CCCS ki*iX(infinity). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, and short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, the short, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node b to node c. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through R1 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(\infty)$]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*] 			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(\infty)$, *-*]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}	
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistor <m>R_2</m> is shorted out and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a voltage source Vs, one resistor, a short, and a CCCS ki*iX(infinity). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, the CCCS, and short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, the short, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node b to node c. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through R1 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(\infty)$]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(\infty)$, *-*]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting the three branches of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= i_X(\infty) + k_ii_X(\infty)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1 + k_i)i_X(\infty)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KVL around the outer loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= R_1 i_X(\infty)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_X(\infty) \amp= \frac{V_S}{R_1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use the two equations to solve for <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= (1 + k_i)\left(\frac{V_S}{R_1}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1+0.22)\left(\frac{9.7~\textrm{V}}{8~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1.22)(1.2125~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.48~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. There are two methods that can be used to calculate this value. The first method is to derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes b and c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 				(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*]			 (2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o]				(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]					(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the open-circuit voltage. Define branch current <m>i_2(t)</m> as the current through resistor <m>R_2</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Branch current i2(t) flows through R2 from node b to node c. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes b and c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*, i^=$i_2(t)$] 	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, -o]						(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]				(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]							(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the left-hand mesh of the circuit to obtain one equation in terms of <m>i_X(t)</m> and <m>i_2(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>R_1i_X(t) + R_2i_2(t) = V_S</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting the three branches of the circuit to derive a second equation in terms of <m>i_X(t)</m> and <m>i_2(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i_X(t) + k_ii_X(t) = i_2(t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The two equations are expressed below with numerical values. The units of V, mA, and <nbsp />k&#8486; are used, but are not explicitly shown.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>8i_X(t) + 60i_2(t) \amp= 9.7</mrow>
							<mrow>1.22i_X(t) - i_2(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>i_X(t)</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>i_2(t)</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								8 	\amp 60		\amp 9.7\\
								1.22	\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0.1195\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0.1457\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The open-circuit voltage is equal to <m>R_2i_2(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OC} \amp=R_2i_2(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (60~\textrm{k}\Omega)(0.1457~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.74~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Short the two terminals of the circuit to calculate the short-circuit current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, a short, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, and the short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, the CCCS, and the short connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Branch current Isc is defined through the short from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 				(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*] 		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]		(7.5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$] ++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]		(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistor <m>R_2</m> is shorted out and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistor R1, a short, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, the CCCS, and the short connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, the CCCS, and the short connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Branch current Isc is defined through the short from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]		(5,2.5) to [short, i^=$I_{SC}$] ++(down:2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]			(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the outer loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= R_1i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_X(t) \amp= \frac{V_S}{R_1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting the three circuit branches.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= i_x(t) + k_ii_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1+k_i)i_X(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use both equations to solve for <m>I_{SC}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= (1+k_i)\left(\frac{V_S}{R_1}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1.22)\left(\frac{9.7~\textrm{V}}{8~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (1.22)(1.2125~\textrm{mA})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.48~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>R_{TH}</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{V_{OC}}{I_{SC}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{8.74~\textrm{V}}{1.48~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.91~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The second method is to derive the differential equation from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of voltage source Vs, resistors R1 and R2, inductor L, and CCCS ki*iX(t). The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node a. R1, R2, the CCCS, and L connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, the CCCS, and L connect at node c. The CCCS flows out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through R1 from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node b to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] 	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$i_X(t)$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*] (2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]		(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]	(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$]	(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]			(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KVL around the outer loop of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-kvl">V_S = R_1i_X(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL at the node that connects the four circuit branches. Note that the voltage dropped over resistor <m>R_2</m> is the same as the voltage dropped over the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>i_X(t) \amp= \frac{L\frac{di(t)}{dt}}{R_2} - k_ii_X(t) + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_X(t) + k_ii_X(t) \amp= \frac{L}{R_2}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_X(t) (1+k_i) \amp= \frac{L}{R_2}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-chargingrl-example5-kcl">i_X(t) \amp= \frac{L}{R_2(1+k_i)}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{1+k_i}i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-chargingrl-example5-kcl" /> into <xref ref="ch6-chargingrl-example5-kvl" /> and reduce until the differential equation is in standard form.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= R_1\left(\frac{L}{R_2(1+k_i)}\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{1}{1+k_i}i(t)\right) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{R_1L}{R_2(1+k_i)}+L\right) \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R_1}{1+k_i}\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(\frac{L(R_1+R_2(1+k_i))}{R_2(1+k_i)}\right) \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R_1}{1+k_i}\right)i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_SR_2(1+k_i)}{L(R_1+R_2(1+k_i))} \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{R_2R_1}{L(R_1+R_2(1+k_i))}i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>1.066 \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + 0.7209i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This yields the same result as the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit method, as expected. If we had not already derived the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, we could use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to solve for <m>R</m>.</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = 1.48~\textrm{mA}~\left[1-\textrm{e}^{-0.7209t}\right]~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-chargingrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (5.91~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1.48~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-0.7209t}~u(t))</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.7~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-0.7209t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>RL Circuits: General Transient Response</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch6-general-ex1">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="general-rl-example1-diagram" />. The switch is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="general-rl-example1-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-general-ex1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example1-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of a 15mA source, 24V source, four resistors, inductor, and SPST switch. The 15mA source, 2kohm and 4kohm (A) resistors connect at node a. The 15mA source flows into node a. The 4kohm (A), 6kohm, and one end of the SPST switch connect at node b. The other end of the SPST switch and 4kohm (B) connect at node c. The 4kohm (B) connects to the high-potential end of the 24V source at node d. The 6kohm resistor and 240mH inductor connect at node e. The 15mA source, 2kohm resistor, 240mH inductor, and low-potential end of the 24V source connect at node f. The 15mA source flows out of node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node e to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node e to node f. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=15~mA]									(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]										(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=240~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [cute closing switch]							(7,5);
									\draw[]	(7,5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(9.5,5);
									\draw[]	(9.5,5) to [V, l_=24~V]									(9.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(9.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left[3.14~\textrm{mA} - 0.64~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-35000t}\right]~u(t) + 2.5~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 5.4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-35000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 15mA source and three resistors. The 15mA source, 2kohm, and 4kohm resistors connect at node a. The 15mA source flows into node a. The 4kohm and 6kohm resistors connect at node b. The 15mA source, 2kohm and 6kohm resistors connect at node c. The 15mA source flows out of node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the 6kohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=15~mA]						(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, i_=$i(0)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of a 15mA source, 2kohm resistor, and 10kohm resistor. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The 15mA source flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(0) flows through the 10kohm resistor from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=15~mA]						(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~k$\Omega$, i_=$i(0)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(10~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+10~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{20~\textrm{k}\Omega^2}{12~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.67~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 15~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{1.67~\textrm{k}\Omega}{10~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 15mA source, 24V source, and four resistors. The 15mA source, 2kohm and 4kohm (A) resistors connect at node a. The 15mA source flows into node a. The 4kohm (A), 6kohm, and 4kohm (B) resistors connect at node b. The 4kohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 24V source at node c. The 15mA source, 2kohm and 6kohm resistors connect to the low-potential end of the 24V source at node d. Branch current i(infinity) is defined through the 6kohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=15~mA]									(0,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]						(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=24~V]								(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform source transformation on both sources.</p>
					
					<p>The 15<nbsp />mA current source becomes</p>
						
					<p>
						<md>(15~\textrm{mA})(2~\textrm{k}\Omega) = 30~\textrm{V}.</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The 24<nbsp />V source becomes</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{24~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 6~\textrm{mA}.</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 6mA source, and four resistors. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 2kohm resistor at node a. The 2kohm and 4kohm (A) resistors connect at node b. The 4kohm (A), 6kohm, 4kohm (B) resistors and 6mA source connect at node c. The 6mA source flows into node c. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 6kohm, 4kohm (B), and 6mA source connect at node d. The 6mA source flows out of node d. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 6kohm resistor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=30~V]								(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]									(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(10,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]								(10,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the series resistors.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 30V source, 6mA source, and three resistors. The high-potential end of the 30V source connects to the 6kohm (A) resistor at node a. The 6kohm (A), 6kohm (B), 4kohm resistors and 6mA source connect at node b. The 6mA source flows into node b. The low-potential end of the 30V source, 6kohm (B), 4kohm, and 6mA source connect at node c. The 6mA source flows out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 6kohm (B) resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=30~V]									(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]									(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Convert the voltage source to a current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{30~\textrm{V}}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega} = 5~\textrm{mA}</md>
					</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution6">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of a 5mA source, 6mA source, and three resistors (6k, 6k, and 4k). All five circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Both current sources flow out of node b and into node a. Branch current i(infinity) flows through one of the 6kohm resistors from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=5~mA]								(-2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [I, l^=6~mA]									(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two current sources are in parallel and can be combined.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution7">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of an 11mA source and three resistors (6k, 6k, and 4k). All four circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. The current source flows out of node b and into node a. Branch current i(infinity) flows through one of the 6kohm resistors from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=11~mA]								(-2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(0,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]									(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$]							(5,0);
										\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]										(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{1}{\frac{1}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{1}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{1}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.714~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= 11~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{1.71~\textrm{k}\Omega}{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.14~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m>. Deactivate the sources to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example1-solution8">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of four resistors and one inductor. The 2kohm and 4kohm (A) resistors connect at node a. The 4kohm (A), 6kohm, and 4kohm (B) resistors connect at node b. The 6kohm resistor connects to the 240mH inductor at node c. The 2kohm and 4kohm (B) resistors connect to the 240mH inductor at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$]							(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, *-]						(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=240~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, -*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(5,5);
										\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]										(5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (2~\textrm{k}\Omega+4~\textrm{k}\Omega)//4~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{k}\Omega//4~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.4~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8.4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{L}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{.24~\textrm{H}}{8400~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2.86\times10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[3.14~\textrm{mA} - 0.64~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-35000t}\right]~u(t) + 2.5~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltage" /> to calculate <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (8.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)\left[(3.14~\textrm{mA} - 2.5~\textrm{mA})~\textrm{e}^{-35000t}\right]~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.4~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-35000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-general-ex2">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="general-rl-example2-diagram" />. Switch S1 is closed for a very long time before opening at a time of zero seconds. Switch S2 is open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds.</p>

					<figure xml:id="general-rl-example2-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-general-ex2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example2-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of an 8V source, 20V source, four resistors, one inductor and two SPST switches (S1 and S2). The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 4kohm (A) resistor at node a. The 4kohm (A) resistor connects to S1 at node b. S1, the 5kohm and 20kohm resistors, 100mH inductor, and S2 connect at node c. S2 and 4kohm (B) resistor connect at node e. The 4kohm (B) resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node e. The low-potential ends of both sources, 5kohm and 20kohm resistors, and 100mH inductor connect at node f. Voltage v(t) drops over the inductor from node c to node f. Branch current i(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node f. Switch S1 is depicted as opening. Switch S2 is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute opening switch, l_=S1]				(4,2.5);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]						(4,0);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [short]										(9,2.5);
									\draw[]	(6.5,2.5) to [L, l_=100~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(6.5,0);
									\draw[]	(9,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(9,0);
									\draw[]	(9,2.5) to [cute closing switch, l_=S2]					(11,2.5);
									\draw[]	(11,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(13.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(13.5,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]								(13.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(13.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left[ 5~\textrm{mA} - 3~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-20000t}\right]~u(t) + 2~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-20000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short. Switch S1 is closed and switch S2 is open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example2-solution1">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 8V source, 4k resistor, 5k resistor, 20k resistor, and a short. The high-potential end of the 8V source connects to the 4k resistor at node a. The 4k, 5k, and 20k resistors connect to the short at node b. The low-potential end of the 8V source, 5k and 20k resistors, and short connect at node c. Branch current i(0) flows through the short from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$, *-*]		(5,0);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$]		(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 5<nbsp />k&#8486; and 20<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are shorted by the inductor and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="30%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example2-solution2">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of an 8V source and 4kohm resistor wired in series. Branch current i(0) flows through the circuit from the high-potential end of the source to the low-potential end of the source.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]				(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Draw the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short. Switch S1 is open and switch S2 is closed.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example2-solution3">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 20V source, short, and three resistors. The 5kohm, 20kohm, and 4kohm resistors connect to the short at node a. The 4kohm resistor connects to the high-potential end of the 20V source at node b. The 5kohm and 20kohm resistors, short, and low-potential end of the 20V source connect at node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the short from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$]					(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]					(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 5<nbsp />k&#8486; and 20<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are shorted by the inductor and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="40%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example2-solution4">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 1 mesh consisting of a 20V source and 4kohm resistor both wired in series. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the circuit from the high-potential end of the source to the low-potential end.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$]	(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]		(2.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]			(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{20~\textrm{V}}{4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t=0^+</m> and deactivate the source to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
							<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-generalrl-example2-solution5">
								<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of a 5kohm resistor, 20kohm resistor, 4kohm resistor, and 100mH inductor. All four circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
								<latex-image>
									\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$]							(0,0);
										\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=100~mH, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~k$\Omega$, *-*]					(5,0);
										\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=4~k$\Omega$]							(7.5,2.5);
										\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]									(7.5,0);
										\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
									\end{tikzpicture}
								</latex-image>
  							</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 5~\textrm{k}\Omega//20~\textrm{k}\Omega//4~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp=	2~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{L}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{.1~\textrm{H}}{2000~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5\times10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> to calculate <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left[ 5~\textrm{mA} - 3~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-20000t}\right]~u(t) + 2~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltage" /> to calculate <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(5~\textrm{mA}-2~\textrm{mA})~\textrm{e}^{-20000t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(3~\textrm{mA})~\textrm{e}^{-20000t}~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-20000t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-general-ex3">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-general-example3-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The inductor has a parasitic resistance of 200<nbsp />&#8486;, which is not shown on the circuit diagram.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="rl-general-example3-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-general-ex3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-rl-general-example3-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of an 18A source, three resistors, one inductor, and an SPST switch. The 18A source, 400ohm resistor, and SPST switch connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The SPST switch and 500ohm resistor connect at node b. The 500ohm and 600ohm resistors connect to the 9mH inductor at node c. The 18A source, 400ohm resistor, 600ohm resistor, and 9mH inductor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node c to node d. Branch current iL(t) flows through the inductor from node c to node d. The SPST switch is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=18~A] 											(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]												(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cute closing switch]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] 								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]											(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [L, l_=9~mH, i^=$i_L(t)$]							(10,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= (1.72~\textrm{A} + 3.09~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-62200t})~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (1030~\textrm{V} + 1850~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-62200t})~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>In the initial steady-state, the 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is not connected to any source and has current and voltage values of zero. However, the current through the resistor may not be continuous with time. The initial current through the inductor, <m>i_L(0)</m>, which is also equal to zero, will be continuous when the switch closes. Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the value of <m>i(0^+)</m>. The inductor can be treated as a 0<nbsp />A source. (The parasitic resistance is shown on the diagram below.)</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example3-solution1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 18A source, 0A source, and four resistors. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor connects to the 0A source at node c. The 0A source flows out of node c. The 18A source, 400ohm and 600ohm resistors, and 0A source connect at node d. The 18A source flows out of node d. The 0A source flows into node d. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=18~A] 											(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]												(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] 								(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]												(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$]									(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [I, l_=0~A]										(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]												(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The branch containing the 0<nbsp />A source does not contribute to the circuit and can be removed.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example3-solution2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 18A source and three resistors. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node b. The 18A source, 400ohm and 600ohm resistors connect at node c. The 18A source flows out of node c. Voltage v(0+) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(0+) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=18~A] 										(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] 							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, v^=$v(0^+)$, i_=$i(0^+)$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance of the circuit (as seen by the current source) consists of the 400<nbsp />&#8486; resistor in parallel with the series combination of the 500<nbsp />&#8486; and 600<nbsp />&#8486; resistors. Calculate this equivalent resistance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(1100~\Omega)(400~\Omega)}{1100~\Omega + 400~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{440000~\Omega^2}{1500~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 293.3333~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0^+)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0^+) \amp= 18~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{293.3333~\Omega}{1100~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.8~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by its parasitic resistance.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example3-solution3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 18A source and four resistors. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 18A source, 400ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node c. The 18A source flows out of node c. Voltage v(infinity) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=18~A] 													(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]														(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]									(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] 										(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, v^=$v(\infty)$, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]														(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$]										(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]														(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. Refer to <xref ref="ch2-currentdivider-threemesh-example" /> on using the current divider in a three-mesh circuit in <xref ref="ch2-currentdivider" /> if you're uncertain how the equation below was derived.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= 18~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{400~\Omega // (500~\Omega + (600~\Omega // 200~\Omega))}{500~\Omega + (600~\Omega // 200~\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{600~\Omega // 200~\Omega}{600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{400~\Omega // (500~\Omega + 150~\Omega)}{500~\Omega + 150~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{150~\Omega}{600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{400~\Omega // 650~\Omega}{650~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{150~\Omega}{600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\textrm{A}\left(\frac{247.619~\Omega}{650~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{150~\Omega}{600~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 18~\textrm{A}(0.381)(0.25)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.715~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit at <m>t = 0^+</m> to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The parasitic resistance is explicitly shown in the diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example3-solution4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of an 18A source, four resistors, and one inductor. The 18A source, 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 18A source flows into node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor connects to the 9mH inductor at node c. The 18A source, 400ohm and 600ohm resistors, and 9mH inductor connect at node d. The 18A source flows out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=18~A] 										(0,5);
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [short]											(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] 							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]											(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=9~mH]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Deactivate the current source by replacing it with an open.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example3-solution5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of an 18A source, four resistors, and one inductor. The 400ohm and 500ohm resistors connect at node a. The 500ohm, 600ohm, and 200ohm resistors connect at node b. The 200ohm resistor connects to the 9mH inductor at node c. The 400ohm and 600ohm resistors and 9mH inductor connect at node d. Voltage v(t) drops over the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through the 600ohm resistor from node b to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=500~$\Omega$] 							(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l_=600~$\Omega$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [short]											(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [L, l_=9~mH]									(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]											(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= ((400~\Omega + 500~\Omega) // 600~\Omega) + 200~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (900~\Omega // 600~\Omega) + 200~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 360~\Omega + 200~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 560~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.009~\textrm{H}}{560~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.61 \times 10^{-5}~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(1.72~\textrm{A} + 3.09~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-62200t}\right)~u(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= (600~\Omega)\left(1.72~\textrm{A} + 3.09~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-62200t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \left(1030~\textrm{V} + 1850~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-62200t}\right)~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-general-ex4">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-general-example4-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The parameter values are <m>V_{S1} = 5~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>V_{S2} = 10~\textrm{V}</m>, <m>R_1 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R_3 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 500~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>g_m = 0.5~\textrm{A/V}</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="rl-general-example4-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-general-ex4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-rl-general-example4-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 6 nodes consisting of two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2), a VCCS (gm*vX(t)), three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), and inductor (L), and an SPST switch. R1 and L connect at node a. L, the VCCS, and R2 connect at node b. The VCCS flows out of node b. R2, R3, and the SPST connect at node c. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node d. The SPST and the high-potential end of Vs1 connect at node e. R1, the VCCS, and low-potential end of both voltage sources connect at node f. The VCCS flows into node f. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node b to node a. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node a. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l_=$R_1$] 									(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, mirror]	(0,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(t)$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(t)$] 					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [cute closing switch, *-]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, -*]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]									(7.5,5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left(0.229~\textrm{A} + 0.294~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-43.6t}\right)~u(t) + 0.423~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= -4.23~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-43.6t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in its initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), a VCCS (gm*vX(0)), and voltage source (Vs2). R1, the VCCS, and R2 connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. R2 and R3 connect at node b. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node c. R1, the low-potential end of Vs2, and the VCCS connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node e. Branch current i(0) flows through R1 from node a to node d. Voltage vX(0) drops over R2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$] 				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(0)$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(0)$] (5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}	
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>It is not beneficial to combine resistors <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m> because the voltage drop over <m>R_2</m> dictates the value of the voltage-controlled current source. For this reason, it's also not advantageous to perform source transformation to solve the circuit. KCL and KVL can be used to obtain two equations in terms of two unknowns (<m>i(0)</m> and <m>v_X(0)</m>). First, perform KCL at the node connecting the three branches of the circuit. (Note that the voltage dropped over <m>R_1</m> is equal to <m>R_1i(0)</m>.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>\frac{V_{S2} - R_1i(0)}{R_2 + R_3} = g_mv_X(0) + i(0)</md> 
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the entire outer loop of the circuit. (Note that the current through resistors <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m> is equal to <m>v_X(0) / R_2</m>.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>V_{S2} = -\frac{v_X(0)}{R_2}(R_2 + R_3) + R_1i(0)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug numerical quantities into each equation. The units used for each equation below are V, A, and &#8486; and are not explicitly shown.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{10-20i(0)}{40} = 0.5v_X(0) + i(0)</mrow>
							<mrow>10 = -\frac{40}{20}v_X(0) + 20i(0)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>i(0)</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>v_X(0)</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1.5 	\amp 0.5	\amp 0.25	\\
								20		\amp -2		\amp 10
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0.4231\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp -0.7692\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that <m>i(0) = 0.423~\textrm{A}</m>.</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition to calculate <m>i(\infty)</m>. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2) and a VCCS (gm*vX(infinity)). R1, R2, and the VCCS connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. R2, R3, and the high-potential end of Vs1 connect at node b. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node c. R1, the VCCS, and the low-potential end of both voltage sources connect at node d. The VCCS flows into node d. Branch current i(infinity) flows through R1 from node a to node d. Voltage vX(infinity) drops over R2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$] 						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$]			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(\infty)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(\infty)$] 	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, *-*]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]					(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}	
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>For the same reasons as above, source transformation is not advantageous in solving this circuit. KCL and KVL can again be used instead. Perform KCL at the node connecting <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and the voltage-controlled current source. (Note that the voltage dropped over <m>R_1</m> is equal to <m>R_1i(\infty)</m>.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{V_{S1} - R_1i(\infty)}{R_2} = g_mv_X(\infty) + i(\infty)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the loop containing <m>V_{S1}</m> and resistors <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>V_{S1} = -v_X(\infty) + R_1i(\infty)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug numerical quantities into each equation. The units used for each equation below are V, A, and &#8486; and are not explicitly shown.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{5-20i(\infty)}{20} = 0.5v_X(\infty) + i(\infty)</mrow>
							<mrow>5 = -v_X(\infty) + 20i(\infty)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>i(\infty)</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>v_X(\infty)</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								2	 	\amp 0.5	\amp 0.25	\\
								20		\amp -1		\amp 5
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0.2292\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp -0.4167\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This means that <m>i(\infty) = 0.229~\textrm{A}</m>.</p>

					<p>To calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor, two methods can be used. The first is to derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2) and a VCCS (gm*vX(t)). R2 and the VCCS connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. R2, R3, and the high-potential end of Vs1 connect at node c. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node d. R1, the VCCS, and the low-potential end of both voltage sources connect at node e. The VCCS flows into node e. R1 connects at node b. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node a to node c. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=$R_1$, o-] 					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, o-]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(t)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(t)$] 	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}	
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Mesh analysis is likely the most effective method for solving this circuit. The circuit is re-drawn below to show the two mesh currents.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution4">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit diagram as above. Mesh current IA flows through the VCCS, R2, and Vs1 through nodes e, a, c, and back to e. Mesh current IB flows through Vs1, R3, and Vs2 through nodes e, c, d, and back to e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=$R_1$, o-] 					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, o-]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(t)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(t)$] 	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.3) arc (-60:170:0.3);
									\draw[Stealth-] (6.25,1.25)node{$I_{B}$}  ++(-60:0.3) arc (-60:170:0.3);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Relate branch current <m>I_A</m> to the value of the current source contained entirely within that single mesh.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_A = -g_mv_X(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand loop.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-V_{S1} + R_3I_B + V_{S2} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation relating the controlling voltage to the mesh currents.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_X(t) = I_AR_2</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug numerical quantities into each of the equations. The units used for each equation below are V, A, and &#8486; and are not explicitly shown.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_A \amp= -0.5v_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>-5 + 20I_B + 10 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= 20I_A</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>v_X(t)</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0	 	\amp 0.5	\amp 0\\
								0 	\amp 20		\amp 0		\amp -5\\
								-20	\amp 0		\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 0\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp -0.25\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KVL around the entire outer loop of the circuit to calculate the open-circuit voltage. (Note that resistor <m>R_1</m> does not contribute to the open-circuit voltage, as it has no current flowing through it.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OC} \amp= v_X(t) + I_BR_3 + V_{S2}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0 + (20~\Omega)(-0.25~\textrm{A}) + 10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Short the two terminals to derive the short-circuit current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2), a short, and a VCCS (gm*vX(t)). The short, R2 and the VCCS connect at node a. The VCCS flows out of node a. R2, R3, and the high-potential end of Vs1 connect at node c. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node d. R1, the VCCS, and the low-potential end of both voltage sources connect at node e. The VCCS flows into node e. R1 connects to the short at node b. Voltage vX(t) drops over R2 from node a to node c. Branch current Isc flows through the short from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=$R_1$] 						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(t)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(t)$] 	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Again, mesh analysis is the most direct method for solving this circuit. The mesh currents are shown below. (Note that some component values have been hidden so they don't overlap with mesh current labels.)</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution6">
							<shortdescription>Same circuit diagram as above. Mesh current IA flows through the short, VCCS, and R1 from node b and a back to b. Mesh current IB flows through the VCCS, R2, and Vs1 from node e, a, c, back to e. Mesh current IC flows through Vs1, R3, and Vs2 from node e, c, d, back to e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=$R_1$] 						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, *-*]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(t)$] 	(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.3) arc (-60:170:0.3);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.3) arc (-60:170:0.3);
									\draw[Stealth-] (6.25,1.25)node{$I_C$}  ++(-60:0.3) arc (-60:170:0.3);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Relate the voltage-controlled current source to mesh currents <m>I_A</m> and <m>I_B</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>g_mv_X(t) = I_A - I_B</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the supermesh consisting of meshes A and B.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>R_2I_B + V_{S1} + R_1I_A = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Perform KVL around the right-hand mesh.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>-V_{S1} + R_3I_C + V_{S2} = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Derive an equation relating the controlling voltage to the mesh currents.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>v_X(t) = R_2I_B</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug numerical quantities into each of the equations. The units used for each equation below are V, A, and &#8486; and are not explicitly shown.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0.5v_X(t) \amp= I_A - I_B</mrow>
							<mrow>20I_B + 5 + 20I_A \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>-5 + 20I_C + 10 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>v_X(t) \amp= 20I_B</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The four equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>I_C</m>, the next column contains coefficients of <m>v_X(t)</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp -1		\amp 0	 	\amp -0.5	\amp 0\\
								20	\amp 20		\amp 0		\amp 0		\amp -5\\
								0 	\amp 0		\amp 20		\amp 0		\amp -5\\
								0	\amp 20		\amp 0		\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 	\amp 0		\amp -0.2292\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 0		\amp -0.0208\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 0		\amp -0.25\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp -0.4167
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The short-circuit current is equal to <m>-I_A</m>, which is 0.229<nbsp />A. Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{TH} \amp= \frac{5~\textrm{V}}{0.2292~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 21.8182~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.5~\textrm{H}}{21.8182~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0229~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The second method for solving the circuit is to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example4-solution7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), two voltage sources (Vs1 and Vs2), an inductor (L), and a VCCS (gm*vX(t)). R1 and L connect at node a. L, the VCCS, and R2 connect at node b. The VCCS flows out of node b. R2, R3, and the high-potential end of Vs1 connect at node c. R3 and the high-potential end of Vs2 connect at node d. R1, the VCCS, and the low-potential end of both voltage sources connect at node e. The VCCS flows into node e. Voltage v(t) flows through L from node b to node a. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node a. Voltage vX(t) flows through R2 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$] 								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, mirror]	(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [cI, l_=$g_mv_X(t)$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, v_=$v_X(t)$] 				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S1}$, *-*]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_{S2}$]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}	
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use KCL at the node connecting <m>R_2</m>, <m>L</m>, and the voltage-controlled current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{v_X(t)}{R_2} + g_mv_X(t) + i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-rl-general-example4-kvl">\left(\frac{1}{R_2}+g_m\right)v_X(t) + i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KVL around the loop consisting of <m>V_{S1}</m>, <m>R_2</m>, <m>L</m>, and <m>R_1</m> to derive an equation for <m>v_X(t).</m> (Note that the voltage dropped over <m>R_1</m> is equal to <m>R_1i(t)</m>.)</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>V_{S1} \amp= -v_X(t) + L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-rl-general-example4-vx">v_X(t) \amp= L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t) - V_{S1}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-rl-general-example4-vx" /> into <xref ref="ch6-rl-general-example4-kvl" /> and reduce to standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{1}{R_2}+g_m\right)\left(L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + R_1i(t) - V_{S1}\right) + i(t) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{L}{R_2} + Lg_m\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R_1}{R_2} + R_1g_m + 1\right) i(t) - \left(\frac{V_{S1}}{R_2} + V_{S1}g_m\right) \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\left(\frac{L}{R_2} + Lg_m\right)\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{R_1}{R_2} + R_1g_m + 1\right) i(t) \amp= \left(\frac{V_{S1}}{R_2} + V_{S1}g_m\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + \frac{\left(\frac{R_1}{R_2} + R_1g_m + 1\right)}{\left(\frac{L}{R_2} + Lg_m\right)} i(t) \amp= \frac{\left(\frac{V_{S1}}{R_2} + V_{S1}g_m\right)}{\left(\frac{L}{R_2} + Lg_m\right)}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in numerical quantities, using base units for each value.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>\frac{di(t)}{dt} + 43.64i(t) = 10</md>
					</p>

					<p>This value agrees with the value calculated using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, as expected.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(0.229~\textrm{A} + 0.194~\textrm{A}~\textrm{e}^{-43.6t}\right)~u(t) + 0.423~\textrm{A}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>. Recall that the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor is 21.8182<nbsp />&#8486;. (If you did not solve for <m>\tau</m> using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> with the value of <m>\tau</m> derived from the differential equation and solve for <m>R</m>.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = -4.23~\textrm{V}~\textrm{e}^{-43.6t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch6-general-ex5">
				<statement>
					<p>Derive equations for <m>i(t)</m> and <m>v(t)</m> given the circuit shown in <xref ref="rl-general-example5-diagram" />. The switch has been open for a very long time before closing at a time of zero seconds. The parameter values are <m>I_{S1} = 5.5~\textrm{mA}</m>, <m>I_{S2} = 2.6~\textrm{mA}</m>, <m>R_1 = 68~\Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 20~\Omega</m>, <m>R_3 = 14~\Omega</m>, <m>L = 18~\textrm{mH}</m>, and <m>k_i = 0.2</m>.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="rl-general-example5-diagram">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch6-general-ex5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-rl-general-example5-diagram">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of two current sources (Is1 and Is2), three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), an SPST, an inductor (L), and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)). Is1, R1, and R2 connect at node a. Is1 flows into node a. R2, L, and the SPST connect at node b. The SPST, R3, CCCS, and Is2 connect at node c. The CCCS and Is2 both flow into node c. Is1, R1, L, R3, the CCCS, and Is2 connect at node d. All three current sources flow out of node d. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node b to node d. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node b to node d. Branch current iX(t) flows through the SPST from node b to node c. The SPST is depicted as closing.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S1}$] 								(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]										(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [cute closing switch, i_=$i_X(t)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]									(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]						(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.5,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S2}$]							(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]										(12.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(t) \amp= \left(6.42~\textrm{mA} - 2.17~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-784t}\right)~u(t) + 4.25~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</mrow>
							<mrow>v(t) \amp= 30.6~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-784t}~u(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Analyze the circuit in the initial steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution1">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of current source Is1, resistor R1, and resistor R2. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Is1 flows out of node b into node a. Branch current i(0) flows through R2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S1}$] 			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(0)$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit as seen by the current source.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(68~\Omega)(20~\Omega)}{68~\Omega + 20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{1360~\Omega^2}{88~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 15.4545~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>i(0)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>i(0) \amp= 5.5~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{15.4545~\Omega}{20~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.25~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Analyze the circuit in the final steady-state condition. The inductor can be replaced by a short.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution2">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 5 meshes consisting of two current sources (Is1 and Is2), three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), a CCCS (ki*iX(infinity)), and two shorts (SH1 and SH2). Is1, R1, and R2 connect at node a. Is1 flows into node a. R2, SH1, and SH2 connect at node b. SH2, R3, the CCCS, and Is2 connect at node c. The CCCS and Is2 both flow into node c. Is1, R1, SH1, R3, the CCCS, and Is2 connect at node d. All three current sources flow out of node d. Branch current i(infinity) flows through SH1 from node b to node d. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through SH2 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S1}$] 					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(\infty)$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]						(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(\infty)$, *-*]	(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.5,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S2}$]				(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(12.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistor <m>R_3</m> is shorted out and can be removed from the circuit. In addition, the two current sources on the right-hand side of the circuit can be combined into one source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution3">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of one current source (Is1), two resistors (R1 and R2), a CCCS (ki*iX(infinity)+Is2), and two shorts (SH1 and SH2). Is1, R1, and R2 connect at node a. Is1 flows into node a. R2, SH1, and SH2 connect at node b. SH2 and the CCCS connect at node c. The CCCS flows into node c. Is1, R1, SH1, and the CCCS connect at node d. Both current sources flow out of node d. Branch current i(infinity) flows through SH1 from node b to node d. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through SH2 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S1}$] 						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(\infty)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(\infty) + I_{S2}$]	(7.5,2.5);	
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transform <m>I_{S1}</m> into a voltage source of value <m>R_1I_{S1}</m>.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution4">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of one voltage source (R1*Is1), two resistors (R1 and R2), a CCCS (ki*iX(infinity)+Is2), and two shorts (SH1 and SH2). The high-potential end of R1*Is1 and R1 connect at node a. R1 and R2 connect at node b. R2, SH1, and SH2 connect at node c. SH2 and the CCCS connect at node d. The CCCS flows into node d. The low-potential end of R1*Is1, SH1, and the CCCS connect at node e. The CCCS flows out of node e. Branch current i(infinity) flows through SH1 from node c to node e. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through SH2 from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$R_1I_{S1}$] 					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(\infty)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(\infty) + I_{S2}$]	(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two resistors in series and then perform another source transformation back to a current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="90%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution5">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 3 meshes consisting of one independent current source (R1*Is1/(R1+R2)), one resistor (R1+R2), a CCCS (ki*iX(infinity)+Is2), and two shorts (SH1 and SH2). The independent current source, R1+R2, SH1, and SH2 connect at node a. The independent current source flows into node a. SH2 and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Both current sources, SH1, and R1+R2 connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through SH1 from node a to node c. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through SH2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$R_1I_{S1}/(R_1+R_2)$] 			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-*] 			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(\infty)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(\infty) + I_{S2}$]	(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The <m>R_1 + R_2</m> resistor is shorted out.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution6">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of one independent current source (R1*Is1/(R1+R2)), a CCCS (ki*iX(infinity)+Is2), and two shorts (SH1 and SH2). The independent current source, SH1, and SH2 connect at node a. The independent current source flows into node a. SH2 and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Both current sources and SH1 connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Branch current i(infinity) flows through SH1 from node a to node c. Branch current iX(infinity) flows through SH2 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$R_1I_{S1}/(R_1+R_2)$] 			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i(\infty)$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(\infty)$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(\infty) + I_{S2}$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Because the controlling current <m>i_X(\infty)</m> is situated between the two current sources, the two current sources cannot be added together, even though they are in parallel. Instead, use KCL twice, once at each node, to obtain two equations in terms of the two unknowns <m>i(\infty)</m> and <m>i_X(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-example5-kcl1">\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1 + R_2} \amp= i(\infty) + i_X(\infty)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-example5-kcl2">i_X(\infty) + k_ii_X(\infty) + I_{S2} \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve <xref ref="ch6-example5-kcl2" /> for <m>i_X(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>(1+k_i)i_X(\infty) \amp= -I_{S2}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-example5-kcl2rearr">i_X(\infty) \amp= -\frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-example5-kcl2rearr" /> into <xref ref="ch6-example5-kcl1" /> and solve for <m>i(\infty)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1 + R_2} \amp= i(\infty) - \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
							<mrow>i(\infty) \amp= \frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1 + R_2} + \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in numerical quantities to calculate that <m>i(\infty) = 6.4167~\textrm{mA}</m>.</p>

					<p>There are two methods that can be used to calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor. The first method that will be used is to derive the Th&#233;venin equivalent circuit as seen by the inductor.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution7">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 4 nodes consisting of two current sources (Is1 and Is2), three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), a short, and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)). Is1, R1, and R2 connect at node a. Is1 flows into node a. R2 and the short connect at node b. The short, R3, Is2, and the CCCS connect at node c. Both Is2 and the CCCS flow into node c. All three current sources, R1, and R3 connect at node d. All three current sources flow out of node d. Branch current iX(t) flows through the short from node b to node c. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes b and d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S1}$] 			(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]				++(down:0.75);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]				++(up:0.75);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(t)$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]				(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l^=$k_ii_X(t)$, *-*]	(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(12.5,0) to [I, l^=$I_{S2}$]		(12.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(12.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Source transform <m>I_{S1}</m> into a voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution8">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 nodes consisting of one voltage source (R1*Is1), three resistors (R1, R2, and R3), a short, and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)+Is2). The high-potential end of R1*Is1 and R1 connect at node a. R1 and R2 connect at node b. R2 and the short connect at node c. The short, R3, and the CCCS connect at node d. The CCCS flows into node d. The low-potential end of R1*Is1, the CCCS, and R3 connect at node e. The CCCS flows out of node e. Branch current iX(t) flows through the short from node c to node d. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes c and e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$R_1I_{S1}$] 			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$] 				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					++(down:0.75);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]					++(up:0.75);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(t)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]					(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(t) + I_{S2}$]	(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the two series resistors and transform back into a current source.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution9">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes consisting of one independent current source (R1*Is1/(R1+R2)), two resistors (R1+R2 and R3), a short, and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)+Is2). The independent current source, R1+R2, and the short connect at node a. The short, R3, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Both current sources, R1+R2, and R3 connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through the short from node a to node b. The Thevenin equivalent circuit is defined between nodes a and c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$R_1I_{S1}/(R_1+R_2)$] 	(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					++(down:0.75);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]					++(up:0.75);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(t)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]					(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(t) + I_{S2}$]	(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>For the same reason as above, the two current sources cannot be combined into a single current source. The open-circuit voltage is the voltage that drops over either <m>R_1 + R_2</m> or <m>R_3</m>. The current through these two branches will therefore be equal to <m>V_{OC}/(R_1+R_2)</m> and <m>V_{OC}/R_3</m>, respectively. Use KCL at the two nodes between <m>i_X(t)</m> to obtain two equations in terms of <m>V_{OC}</m> and <m>i_x(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-thev-kcl1">\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} \amp= \frac{V_{OC}}{R_1+R_2} + i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-thev-kcl2">i_X(t) + I_{S2} + k_ii_X(t) \amp= \frac{V_{OC}}{R_3}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve <xref ref="ch6-general5-thev-kcl2" /> for <m>i_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>i_X(t) + k_ii_X(t) \amp= -I_{S2} + \frac{V_{OC}}{R_3}</mrow>
							<mrow>(1+k_i)i_X(t) \amp= -I_{S2} + \frac{V_{OC}}{R_3}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-thev-kcl2ix">i_X(t) \amp= \frac{-I_{S2}}{1+k_i} + \frac{V_{OC}}{R_3(1+k_i)}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-general5-thev-kcl2ix" /> into <xref ref="ch6-general5-thev-kcl1" /> and reduce.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} \amp= \frac{V_{OC}}{R_1+R_2} - \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i} + \frac{V_{OC}}{R_3(1+k_i)}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i} \amp= \frac{V_{OC}}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{V_{OC}}{R_3(1+k_i)}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug numerical values into the equation and solve for <m>V_{OC}</m>. Units, which are not explicitly shown, are mV, mA, and &#8486;.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>4.25 + 2.1667 \amp= \frac{V_{OC}}{88} + \frac{V_{OC}}{16.8}</mrow>
							<mrow>16.4167 \amp= \frac{16.8V_{OC} + 88V_{OC}}{1478.4}</mrow>
							<mrow>9486.4 \amp= 104.8V_{OC}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_{OC} \amp= 90.519~\textrm{mV}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Short the two terminals to calculate the short-circuit current.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution10">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of one independent current source (R1*Is1/(R1+R2)), two resistors (R1+R2 and R3), two shorts (SH1 and SH2), and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)+Is2). The independent current source, R1+R2, SH1, and SH2 connect at node a. The independent current source flows into node a. SH2, R3, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Both current sources, SH1, R1+R2, and R3 connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through SH2 from node a to node b. Branch current Isc flows through SH1 from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$R_1I_{S1}/(R_1+R_2)$] 	(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(t)$]			(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]			(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]					(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(t) + I_{S2}$]	(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Both parallel resistors are shorted and can be removed from the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution11">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 2 meshes consisting of one independent current source (R1*Is1/(R1+R2)), two shorts (SH1 and SH2), and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)+Is2). The independent current source, SH1, and SH2 connect at node a. The independent current source flows into node a. SH2, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Both current sources and SH1 connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through SH2 from node a to node b. Branch current Isc flows through SH1 from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$R_1I_{S1}/(R_1+R_2)$] 		(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$I_{SC}$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(t)$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(t) + I_{S2}$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Branch current <m>i_X(t)</m> must be equal to the negative value of the source <m>I_{S2} + k_ii_X(t)</m>. Use this to derive an equation for <m>i_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>i_X(t) \amp= -I_{S2} - k_ii_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>i_X(t) + k_ii_X(t) \amp= -I_{S2}</mrow>
							<mrow>(1+K_i)i_X(t) \amp= -I_{S2}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-isc-ix">i_X(t) \amp= \frac{-I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use KCL at either node to obtain an equation relating <m>I_{SC}</m> and <m>i_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="ch6-general5-isc-kcl">\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} = I_{SC} + i_X(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-general5-isc-ix" /> into <xref ref="ch6-general5-isc-kcl" /> and solve for <m>I_{SC}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} \amp= I_{SC} - \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= \frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in numerical quantities to solve for <m>I_SC</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{SC} \amp= 4.25~\textrm{mA} + 2.1667~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.4167~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the Th&#233;venin equivalent resistance, which is equal to the equivalent resistance as seen by the inductor.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{90.519~\textrm{mV}}{6.4167~\textrm{mA}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 14.1069~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> to calculate <m>\tau</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\tau \amp= \frac{0.018~\textrm{H}}{14.1069~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0013~\textrm{s}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The second method to solve this circuit is to derive the differential equation.</p>

					<figure>
						<caption></caption>
						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch6-general-rl-example5-solution12">
							<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 nodes and 4 meshes consisting of one independent current source (R1*Is1/(R1+R2)), two resistors (R1+R2 and R3), one short, an inductor (L), and a CCCS (ki*iX(t)+Is2). The independent current source, R1+R2, L, and the short connect at node a. The independent current source flows into node a. The short, R3, and the CCCS connect at node b. The CCCS flows into node b. Both current sources, L, R1+R2, and R3 connect at node c. Both current sources flow out of node c. Branch current iX(t) flows through the short from node a to node b. Voltage v(t) drops over L from node a to node c. Branch current i(t) flows through L from node a to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$R_1I_{S1}/(R_1+R_2)$] 				(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]									(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [L, l_=$L$, v^=$v(t)$, i_=$i(t)$, *-*]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, i_=$i_X(t)$]						(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]						(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [short]								(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(10,0) to [cI, l_=$k_ii_X(t) + I_{S2}$]				(10,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(10,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Perform KCL at both nodes surrounding <m>i_X(t)</m> to obtain two equations.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-diffeq-kcl1">\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_1+R_2} + i(t) + i_X(t)</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-diffeq-kcl2">i_X(t) + I_{S2} + k_ii_X(t) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_3}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Solve <xref ref="ch6-general5-diffeq-kcl2" /> for <m>i_X(t)</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>i_X(t) (1+k_i) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_3} - I_{S2}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-diffeq-kcl2ix">i_X(t) \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_3(1+k_i)} - \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug <xref ref="ch6-general5-diffeq-kcl2ix" /> into <xref ref="ch6-general5-diffeq-kcl1" /> and combine like terms.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow>\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} \amp= \frac{v(t)}{R_1+R_2} + i(t) + \frac{v(t)}{R_3(1+k_i)} - \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="ch6-general5-diffeq-kclreduced">\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i} \amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3(1+k_i)}\right)v(t) + i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The relationship between <m>v(t)</m> and <m>i(t)</m> is defined by <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" />. Plug <xref ref="ch6-voltageinductor" /> into <xref ref="ch6-general5-diffeq-kclreduced" /> and put into standard form.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i} \amp= \left(\frac{1}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3(1+k_i)}\right)L\frac{di(t)}{dt} + i(t)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{\frac{R_1I_{S1}}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{I_{S2}}{1+k_i}}{L\left(\frac{1}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3(1+k_i)}\right)} \amp= \frac{di(t)}{dt} + \left(\frac{1}{L\left(\frac{1}{R_1+R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3(1+k_i)}\right)}\right) i(t)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Plug in numerical values, using base units for each quantity.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>5028.8 = \frac{di(t)}{dt} + 783.715i(t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>This value agrees with the value calculated using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, as expected.</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlcurrent" /> to derive an equation for <m>i(t)</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>i(t) = \left(6.42~\textrm{mA} - 2.17~\textrm{mA}~\textrm{e}^{-784t}\right)~u(t) + 4.25~\textrm{mA}~u(-t)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ch6-generalrlvoltage" /> to derive an equation for <m>v(t)</m>. Recall that the equivalent resistance as seen by the capacitor is 14.1069<nbsp />&#8486;. (If you did not solve for <m>\tau</m> using the Th&#233;venin equivalence method, use <xref ref="ch6-taurlequation" /> with the value of <m>\tau</m> derived from the differential equation and solve for <m>R</m>.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>v(t) = 30.6~\textrm{mV}~\textrm{e}^{-784t}~u(t)</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>
	</exercises>

	

</chapter>